function wuaPrepareOpponentCampaign(){
  const o=WUA.opponent;
  const last=(WUA_TICKETS[o]||WUA_TICKETS.Daniels).last;

  const strategy={
    Daniels:"Daniels and Rubio are making the Midwest and the economy the center of their campaign. We can't defend everything at once, and there are places where our own record gives us opportunities. Where do you want us concentrating early?",
    Romney:"Romney and Ryan are making the recovery and federal spending the center of their campaign. They're betting they can turn every voter who's still frustrated with the economy into a referendum on us. Where do you want us concentrating early?",
    Huntsman:"Huntsman and Haley are trying to make this election a referendum on competence and political exhaustion. Their whole pitch is that the country can keep moving without another four years of warfare in Washington. Where do you want us concentrating early?",
    Walker:"Walker is trying to nationalize the fight he started in Wisconsin. He wants this election to be about unions, spending and whether we made government too powerful. Where do you want us concentrating early?",
    Christie:"Christie and Keane are making foreign policy their opening argument. They say four years of restraint has made America look weak and unreliable. Where do you want us concentrating early?",
    Trump:"Trump is turning every rally into a referendum on you personally. He's mixing attacks on the economy, regulation and Washington with the usual insults, and the coverage is following him everywhere. Where do you want us concentrating early?",
    Cain:"Cain is leaning hard into the outsider businessman pitch, arguing that Washington needs somebody who's actually run something and that our recovery proves politicians can't manage the economy. Where do you want us concentrating early?",
    Brewer:"Brewer is making immigration and the border the centerpiece of the race, while Thune is trying to broaden the ticket into a conventional Republican economic campaign. Where do you want us concentrating early?",
    Palin:"Palin and Bachmann are running almost entirely on ideological backlash to this administration. Every speech is socialism, spending, guns, religion and weakness abroad. Where do you want us concentrating early?",
    West:"West and Brewer are treating the campaign like a national emergency, mixing attacks on immigration and national security with accusations that this administration is fundamentally hostile to the Constitution. Where do you want us concentrating early?"
  };

  // The endorsement replaces the campaign-visit slot; no old travel question remains.
  const previous=campaignTrail_temp.player_answers||[];
  const defendedNader=previous.includes(165)||previous.includes(166);
  const pulledNader=previous.includes(167);
  const hawkishEnough=Number(ForeignPolicy)<=-0.85;
  const naderRoute=hawkishEnough && pulledNader;
  const venturaRoute=hawkishEnough && defendedNader;
  if(!naderRoute && !venturaRoute){
    WUA.steinEndorsement=null;
    WUA.venturaAttackAnswer=null;
    // Keep the original campaign-visit question for non-hawkish runs,
    // but ALWAYS continue to the independent Q36 opponent-specific setup below.
  } else {
  const naderA="I gave Ralph a place in this administration when nobody else would, and now he's turning around and doing this? Let's remind people what we've accomplished and how little Ralph has done.";
  const venturaA="Jesse's just bitter he didn't get nominated. Ya know he wanted to be Secretary of Defense? He's not realistic. I'm not going to sit here and pretend this is some principled stand. If he wants to make it personal, we can make it personal.";
  const naderFB="You might have a point, but attacking Nader personally isn't going to make Stein's supporters any more sympathetic to you. The story becomes less about your administration's accomplishments and more about your very public falling-out with Nader, a guy who contrary to what you said has done a lot in his life";
  const venturaFB="The Secretary of Defense line gets plenty of attention, and Ventura is more than happy to fire back. Most reporters want to know why a guy who's out there questioning whether 9/11 was a controlled demolition was ever considered for anything. Stein is enjoying all the free publicity. Saying nothing would've been smarter.";
  const universalB="I understand why some of these people are frustrated with us. But let's remind them what we've actually gotten done. We've spent four years fighting for working people, and there's a hell of a lot more we can accomplish if we get another four. I want to make that case directly.";
  const universalC="I'm not spending the last few weeks of this campaign arguing with a third-party candidate. We've got a Republican nominee to beat. Let Stein have her moment in the spotlight. We're going to keep doing what we've been doing.";
  const universalBFB="Your campaign spends the next several days reminding voters of your administration's accomplishments, particularly on economic issues and workers' rights. Some of Stein's supporters aren't going to be persuaded, especially those who feel you've abandoned your principles on foreign policy. Still, you manage to bring a few of them back into your column.";
  const universalCFB="Your campaign stays focused on the Republican nominee, and the story eventually begins to lose steam. Stein still has a considerably larger following than she did a few weeks ago, but at least you've avoided giving her any more free publicity. Whether those voters come back to you is another question entirely.";
  const naderQuestion="Ralph Nader has announced that he's endorsing Jill Stein for President, citing your administration's less than dovish choices on foreign policy. Nader, says that while he still agrees with much of your domestic agenda, he can no longer support the direction you've taken the country abroad.\n\nThe endorsement has given Stein's campaign a major boost. Recent polls now show her attracting around 4–5% of the national vote, including a considerable number of voters who supported you four years ago. We're not in a safe enough position to totally write this off, either. How do you want to handle it?";
  const venturaQuestion="Jill Stein's campaign is gaining some unexpected traction after receiving an endorsement from Jesse Ventura, the former Minnesota governor whom you briefly considered for Secretary of Veterans Affairs.\n\nVentura says he's disappointed with the direction of your administration and believes Stein offers voters an alternative to the two major parties. His endorsement has attracted considerable media attention, and recent polls now show Stein drawing a more considerable following.\n\nAs a rule it's usually smart to ignore these things. But with this election shaping up to be so close, we should have a strategy.";
  const choiceA=naderRoute?naderA:venturaA;
  wuaSet(1191,naderRoute?naderQuestion:venturaQuestion,[
    {text:choiceA,fb:naderRoute?naderFB:venturaFB},
    {text:universalB,fb:universalBFB},
    {text:universalC,fb:universalCFB}
  ]);
  WUA.steinEndorsement=naderRoute?'Nader':'Ventura';
  const steinAnswers=wuaAnswers(1191);
  // Endorsement baseline is applied directly to Stein's state multipliers once,
  // independent of Dennis's answer. The higher state values OVERRIDE the global boost.
  // Boost only AFTER the endorsement question has been answered (Q1191).
  steinAnswers.forEach((a,i)=>{
    // Remove old campaign-visit scoring and the ineffective old Stein answer scores.
    campaignTrail_temp.answer_score_state_json.filter(r=>Number(r.fields.answer)===Number(a.pk)).forEach(r=>r.fields.state_multiplier=0);
    campaignTrail_temp.answer_score_global_json.filter(r=>Number(r.fields.answer)===Number(a.pk)&&Number(r.fields.affected_candidate)===18).forEach(r=>r.fields.global_multiplier=0);
    wuaGlobal(a.pk,i===0?-0.005:i===1?0.004:0);
  });
  WUA.venturaAttackAnswer=venturaRoute?Number(steinAnswers[0]?.pk):null;
  } // End conditional Stein endorsement; Q36 must be configured on EVERY route.

  const attack={
    Daniels:"Daniels isn't wasting any time going after the heart of our record. He's lumped together the stimulus packages, auto bailout and healthcare bill as evidence of an administration that thinks there's no problem government can't spend its way out of. The line getting repeated everywhere is pretty simple: \"Dennis Kucinich inherited a recession. He didn't have to make it a four-year recovery.\" With unemployment still higher than we'd like, it's not an attack we can just laugh off. How do you want to answer it?",
    Romney:"Romney isn't wasting any time making the recovery his entire case. He's lumped together the stimulus packages, auto rescue and healthcare bill and says we spent four years proving that good intentions don't create jobs. His line is simple: \"Dennis Kucinich inherited a recession. Four years later, too many Americans are still waiting for the recovery.\" With unemployment still higher than we'd like, it's not an attack we can just laugh off. How do you answer it?",
    Huntsman:"Huntsman is making a different argument than most Republicans would. He gives us credit for stabilizing the country, then says we squandered the chance to actually unite it by turning too many issues into ideological fights. He's selling himself as the President who can keep the recovery moving without another four years of trench warfare. How do you answer it?",
    Walker:"Walker isn't even pretending this isn't personal. He's built half his campaign around Wisconsin, telling crowds that when his state tried to get its finances under control, Dennis Kucinich flew in from Washington and sided with the unions against Wisconsin taxpayers. Now he's saying the whole presidency has worked the same way: a monarch-like President hiding behind the power of the Presidency, trying to dictate what states can and can't do for their people. How do you answer him?",
    Christie:"Christie has turned his record in New Jersey into the centerpiece of his campaign. He says he inherited a fiscal mess, made decisions politicians had been avoiding for years and didn't give a damn who yelled at him afterward. Now he's telling voters that's exactly what Washington needs. Keane's covering national security while Christie spends most of his time asking whether Dennis Kucinich has ever met a government program he didn't want to fund. How do you answer him?",
    Trump:"Trump's making this campaign as personal as everyone expected. He says you're a weak negotiator, that China and OPEC have spent four years laughing at you and that Washington politicians like Dennis Kucinich don't have the slightest idea how the real economy works. Then he points to his buildings, his television show and his bank account and asks voters which one of you they'd rather have negotiating for America. Portman is always brought out to clarify what Trump really meant and how he's not as stupid as he sounds. How do you answer him?",
    Cain:"Cain is running straight at our economic record. He says the problem isn't that we didn't spend enough, it's that Washington tried to manage a recovery it didn't understand. He's using 9-9-9 as the contrast and asking whether voters trust a businessman or four more years of federal programs. How do you answer it?",
    Brewer:"Brewer is making Arizona's fight with Washington the centerpiece of her campaign. She says the last four years have been one long lesson in what happens when the federal government refuses to do its job and then tells states they aren't allowed to do it themselves. She's holding up Arizona's immigration law as Exhibit A and saying you care more about telling governors what they can't do than securing the border. How do you answer her?",
    Palin:"Palin is coming out swinging. Every rally is healthcare, guns, abortion, spending, religion and the wars all thrown together before she tells the crowd that you represent everything that's gone wrong with America. Bachmann usually takes it a step further. They're betting they can make this election a referendum on whether the country can even handle 4 more years of you when you're \"tearing down real America\". How do we respond?",
    West:"West has decided subtlety isn't his style. He's calling your economic agenda socialism, your healthcare law unconstitutional and practically every liberal in Congress a communist. Brewer's mostly sticking to immigration while West tells crowds that this election is about whether America is still going to be America four years from now. How do we answer somebody who's attacking almost everything you believe in at once?"
  };

  const q36Special={
    Daniels:[
      {text:"Four years ago we were losing hundreds of thousands of jobs a month, the banks were collapsing and the American auto industry was staring over the edge. Mitch can complain about what we spent. I'll ask people whether they'd rather have the money back and the recession we inherited.",fb:"That's it! That's our message alright. Daniels fires right back with the cost of everything we've done, but he's arguing numbers while we're talking about jobs, healthcare and an auto industry that's still here. I'm comfortable with that."},
      {text:"Mitch Daniels was George Bush's budget director. When he walked into that job, this country had a surplus. When he left, we had a deficit. I'm not taking lectures about fiscal responsibility from the guy who helped write the Bush budgets.",fb:"Daniels immediately reminds everyone that he ran the budget office, not Lehman Brothers, then says four years is plenty of time to start owning your own economy. Alright, that's a good comeback. He was ready for this one."},
      {text:"Mitch Daniels has spent years telling working people in Indiana that if they just trusted him, prosperity would follow. Ask the state employees who lost their jobs when he privatized prison services how that worked out. Some of them had to take steep demotions just to protect what they'd spent years earning. His word doesn't mean anything to me. In Mitch Daniels' America, prosperity stays at the top.",fb:"We got some great testimonials from state employees. One of the guys we interviewed had to become a prison guard to keep his benefits. He called Daniels \"My bitch Mitch\". We will leave that one out."}
    ],
    Romney:[
      {text:"If Mitt wants this election to be about his business career, I'm happy. Let's talk about the companies Bain loaded up with debt, the workers who lost their jobs and the millions he made doing it. He knows how to make money. The question is who benefits when Mitt Romney is in charge.",fb:"His people are screaming that we're attacking free enterprise, but it's pretty hard for him to cosplay as an all-American regular Joe when his private equity firm decimated working people."},
      {text:"Mitt Romney wasn't just a businessman. He was Governor of Massachusetts. Let's look at what actually happened when he had a government to run. He can give all the PowerPoint presentations he wants about what he'd do as President. He's already got a record.",fb:"Romney's people have been expecting this one. Before lunch they've got seventeen different statistics explaining why Massachusetts was actually fantastic. We've got our own statistics, but this quickly becomes a shouting match in the media. Hard to say what effect it'll have."},
      {text:"Mitt Romney wants another twenty-percent income tax cut, a lower corporate rate and fewer rules on Wall Street. We tried that experiment. It ended with taxpayers bailing out the people who ran the economy into the ground. The American people are smarter than this.",fb:"It's a very fair comparison, but Romney spent a good deal of his primary campaign distancing himself from the old guard of the Republican Party. On the issues, he's gonna have a hard time selling himself. But he's been effective at making a lane for himself, so we need to be careful just throwing labels at him."}
    ],
    Huntsman:[
      {text:"Jon's right about one thing: the country is tired of fighting. But we didn't pick these fights for the hell of it. We inherited an economy in free fall, millions without healthcare and two wars. We acted because doing nothing wasn't an option. If Governor Huntsman thinks that was too ideological, he should tell people which part we should've walked away from.",fb:"Smart. Huntsman isn't stupid enough to say we should've let the banks fail or GM go under, so he starts talking about everything that came afterward. Fine. Now he has to explain what exactly he thinks we were supposed to do less of."},
      {text:"Jon Huntsman wants to run as the Republican who isn't like the other Republicans. Alright. Ask him what happens when he gets to Washington with the same Republican Congress we've been dealing with. Is he going to sign their agenda or veto it? Being nicer about it doesn't make the policy any different.",fb:"Good. Huntsman spends the next day explaining all the ways he disagrees with congressional Republicans without actually saying he'd veto their bills. I'm sure John Boehner appreciates the help."},
      {text:"I'm not interested in spending four months arguing about who was nicer to John Boehner. People want to know what the next four years look like. Let's talk about jobs, healthcare, energy and getting wages moving again. If Huntsman wants to campaign on tone, he can campaign on tone.",fb:"Probably smart. Huntsman wanted to spend the week talking about how exhausted everybody is with Washington fighting. Instead, we're talking about jobs again. His rambles about decorum are nice, but they're not helping anyone pay the bills."}
    ],
    Walker:[
      {text:"I went to Wisconsin because working people were having rights they'd held for generations stripped away from them. He can portray me as some out-of-touch elite all he wants to, but we are fighting back. I went to bat for workers because I came from workers. I know what poverty feels like. My family lived in a car, for Christ's sake. Let everyone know where my passion comes from.",fb:"This made for a great ad. Problem is, it doesn't really address the idea that you're overstepping with your authority by getting involved in state issues. Maybe that won't matter, but it's hard to say how effective this will be."},
      {text:"His bill didn't just ask workers to contribute more. He went after collective bargaining itself. Teachers couldn't negotiate their benefits or working conditions anymore. Unions had to keep proving they deserved to exist. If this was only about balancing the books, why was any of that necessary?",fb:"That's the question Walker doesn't particularly want to answer. He says the unions had too much power and somebody finally had the guts to take them on. At least we're done pretending this was just an argument over pension contributions."},
      {text:"I've seen how Scott Walker governs when somebody tells him no. He doesn't negotiate with them. He tries to break them. Maybe that works for him, but the President doesn't get to treat half the country like they're an obstacle that needs to be removed. If that's the kind of President he intends to be, people deserve to know it.",fb:"Walker immediately says that's rich coming from Dennis Kucinich, and he's got four years of fights with Congress to point at. Shit. We might've walked into that one. He's happy to have an argument about which one of you is more stubborn."}
    ],
    Christie:[
      {text:"Chris Christie wants credit for making tough decisions? He killed one of the largest infrastructure projects in the country. Thousands of jobs, better rail service all gone because Chris Christie decided New Jersey couldn't afford to build for the future. That's not leadership. That's called being shortsighted.",fb:"Christie's been waiting for this. He starts rattling off the government's own cost estimates and asks whether you would've personally written New Jersey a blank check, since you don't seem to mind a deficit. We wanted to talk about jobs. Now we're arguing over debt instead."},
      {text:"Chris Christie thinks yelling at teachers makes him tough. I've watched the videos and I've heard the speeches. Being President isn't a town hall where you get to humiliate somebody who disagrees with you and wait for applause. Eventually you've got to sit across the table from people you can't stand and get something done. That's what being tough really looks like.",fb:"Christie says you've spent four years fighting Republicans in Congress, Republican governors and half your own party whenever they got in your way. Then he asks which one of you has the temperament problem. We probably should've seen that coming."},
      {text:"I've noticed something interesting about this ticket. Every time Jack Keane talks about Iran or Syria, it's 'I would do this,' 'I would do that,' 'Here's what I'm going to do.' Well, hold on a second. Chris Christie is running for President. Jack Keane isn't. If they win this election, Christie is going to be Commander in Chief. So who's actually making the decisions on this ticket?",fb:"Keane says you're deliberately twisting his words and Christie makes a big show of saying there's only one Commander in Chief. Still, Keane's spent so much of this campaign talking like he's already running the military that Christie suddenly has to remind everybody who's at the top of the ticket. It's an effective way of getting people to question what's really going on."}
    ],
    Trump:[
      {text:"Let's look at the bankruptcies, the failed businesses and the people who got burned while Donald Trump kept his name on the buildings. He wants to run the country like one of his companies, right? Well, it's the people who will be paying for his screw-ups.",fb:"Nothing you said is untrue, but Trump treats this entire attack like a compliment. He says \"you have to spend money to make money, and you don't reward the lazy people\". How that even gets close to answering you, nobody seems to know."},
      {text:"Donald's right that our trade policy has failed American workers. I've been saying that since before he discovered politics. The difference is I've actually spent my career fighting these trade agreements. Donald Trump spent his career buying whatever made Donald Trump money.",fb:"Trump says that's exactly the problem: you've been in Washington fighting for years and we're still getting ripped off. Then somebody points out how much Chinese steel and glass he's bought for his own buildings. He says that's because our leaders made it impossible to buy American. Somehow, this passes for a real rebuttal. We're probably not going to beat him by agreeing with half his argument."},
      {text:"Every time Donald says something completely insane, Rob Portman comes out twenty minutes later and explains what President Trump apparently meant. If Donald Trump needs a translator while he's running for President, what happens when he's actually President? You really think he can handle a tense negotiation?",fb:"Trump says Portman doesn't translate anything for him, Portman says there's never been any disagreement between them. Then the two of them give different answers about China before the day is over. Keep hitting them on this."}
    ],
    Cain:[
      {text:"Let's make him explain 9-9-9 instead of letting him say it like it's a magic spell. A new national sales tax hits working people every time they buy something. Herman can call that simple if he wants. I call it shifting the tax burden onto people who can least afford it.",fb:"Cain loves talking about 9-9-9 right up until somebody asks who pays the nine percent at the cash register. Now we're talking about groceries and paychecks instead of how easy the numbers are to remember. Good."},
      {text:"He says businessmen understand the economy and politicians don't. Fine. Four years ago GM and Chrysler were staring at bankruptcy and millions of people were losing their jobs. We made decisions. He got to sit on television and tell us what he would've done afterward. There's a difference between running for President and being President.",fb:"Cain's got an easy answer: Washington experience is the problem. Crowd likes that. Fine. Herman got to sit on television while we were trying to keep GM alive. There's a difference, and I think people understand it."},
      {text:"Herman says government caused the problem, but he wants to answer it with another giant tax experiment. Ask him what 9-9-9 would've done in 2009 when people weren't spending, businesses weren't hiring and the auto industry was collapsing. I want specifics, not another slogan.",fb:"There it is. Cain gives one answer, his campaign gives another. This is the exact kind of confusion a Cain presidency would produce. That's where we want him."}
    ],
    Brewer:[
      {text:"Jan Brewer can call it border security all she wants. When you tell police officers to start checking people's immigration status during ordinary stops, American citizens are going to get treated differently because of what they look like or how they speak. I don't want that happening anywhere in this country.",fb:"Brewer says you just accused Arizona cops of being racists and spends the rest of the day demanding an apology. That's not actually what you said, but good luck explaining that to FOX News who run the headline: KUCINICH ATTACKS ARIZONA POLICE. We've got ourselves a fight."},
      {text:"Governor Brewer keeps saying Washington should stay out of Arizona's business. Fine. Does that apply when a state wants tougher environmental rules? Stronger labor protections? More generous healthcare? Or does she only back states' rights when it suits her agenda?",fb:"She says she does, and that states experimenting with different policies means the best ideas \"come out on top\". It's pretty asinine when the issue is people's constitutional rights, but she comes out ahead on this exchange."},
      {text:"I'm tired of hearing politicians use immigration as an excuse to yell at each other. We need secure borders, we need employers held accountable when they exploit undocumented workers, and we need a legal path for people who've built lives here. Jan Brewer can keep campaigning on Arizona's law. I'd rather actually fix the problem.",fb:"Brewer pounces on this, saying if you really wanted to fix it then she wouldn't have to do it herself. It's a good line, and her law is more popular than we'd like to admit. But voters aren't demanding enforcement alone either. There's room for us here. We just need to keep talking about actually fixing the problem."}
    ],
    Palin:[
      {text:"Sarah Palin doesn't get to decide who's a real American and who isn't. Gay couples in California are just as American as steelworkers in Pennsylvania. I'm President of all of them, whether they voted for me or not.",fb:"Palin immediately says you've deliberately twisted what she means by 'real America,' which her people love. Doesn't really matter. We're talking about what kind of country this is instead of whether you secretly hate Christmas. Working people want substance."},
      {text:"Sarah Palin wants to spend this election lecturing everybody about big government? Let's talk about Alaska. Federal money, oil money and pork barrel spending. She was perfectly happy with government spending when it was going to her state. Maybe her objection isn't big government at all, it's who it benefits.",fb:"Palin comes back with a list of spending she vetoed and earmarks she rejected, then says there's a difference between returning taxpayers' money to a state and creating more bureaucracy. She came prepared for this one, and we come out of it looking indecisive."},
      {text:"Sarah Palin is one thing. Michele Bachmann is another. Every time Bachmann says something about anti-American members of Congress or starts another conspiracy theory, Palin tells us that's just Michele being Michele. I'm sorry, but she picked her. If Sarah Palin wants to be President, then Michele Bachmann is one heartbeat away from the presidency. She doesn't get to pretend her own running mate's words don't matter.",fb:"Bachmann responds before Palin does, which is probably the best thing that could've happened to us. She gives a six-minute answer about the media, socialism and vaccines before Palin's campaign finally puts out a statement. Don't interrupt your opponent when they're helping, right?"}
    ],
    West:[
      {text:"Allen West says dozens of members of Congress are communists. Fine. Name them. Show us the evidence. He's running for President of the United States now. If he's going to accuse elected members of Congress of being communists, he ought to be able to prove it.",fb:"West names the Congressional Progressive Caucus and doesn't back down an inch. Problem for him is nobody can find any evidence they're actually communists. Problem for us is that his supporters don't particularly care. At least the majority of Americans see this as ridiculous."},
      {text:"Allen West isn't a conservative. He's a demagogue. Everyone who disagrees with him is a communist, a socialist or somebody trying to destroy America. George Bush was President for eight years and I disagreed with him on damn near everything. I never doubted that he loved his country. Allen West apparently can't extend that courtesy to anybody.",fb:"West says you're proving his point by defending George Bush and immediately starts listing everything you've ever said about the Bush administration. That's cute, but it misses the point. We're not defending Bush's policies. We're reminding people there used to be a difference between disagreeing with somebody and accusing them of trying to destroy America."},
      {text:"Allen West keeps talking like serving in the Army gives him some special authority to decide who loves this country and who doesn't. I respect his service, but the uniform doesn't make your ideas constitutional, and it doesn't make everybody who disagrees with you less American.",fb:"West says you just attacked his military service. You didn't, but he's a decorated combat veteran and now we're spending two days explaining which part of that statement wasn't about his service. There were easier ways to make this point."}
    ]
  };

  const q36Choices=q36Special[o]||q36Special.Daniels;
  wuaSet(1192,attack[o]||attack.Daniels,q36Choices);
  // Explicitly bind the three permanent Q36 answer slots; don't leave the
  // third static Daniels placeholder if an earlier branch parked an answer.
  [1566,1570,2792].forEach((pk,i)=>{
    const a=campaignTrail_temp.answers_json.find(x=>Number(x.pk)===pk);
    if(!a) return;
    a.fields.question=1192;
    a.fields.description=q36Choices[i].text;
    wuaFeedback(pk,q36Choices[i].fb);
  });
  const q36a=wuaAnswers(1192);
  if(q36a.length!==3 || q36a.some(a=>/replaced dynamically/i.test(a.fields.description))){
    console.error("Q36 nominee answer setup failed",o,q36a.map(a=>a.pk));
  }
  const q36Poll={
    Daniels:[Economy>=2.6?.004:Economy>=1.5?.001:-.003,-.003,.002],
    Romney:[.004,0,0],
    Huntsman:[.002,.005,.001],
    Walker:[.001,.005,-.005],
    Christie:[-.003,-.005,.005],
    Trump:[.002,0,.005],
    Cain:[.005,.002,.005],
    Brewer:[-.003,-.005,.005],
    Palin:[.002,-.003,.005],
    West:[.004,.003,-.005]
  };
  const q36m=q36Poll[o]||q36Poll.Daniels;
  q36a.forEach((x,i)=>wuaGlobal(x.pk,q36m[i]||0));
  if(o==='Daniels'&&q36a[2]) wuaState(q36a[2].pk,WUA_STATE.IN,.010);
  if(o==='Romney'&&q36a[0]) ['MI','OH','WI','PA','IN'].forEach(st=>wuaState(q36a[0].pk,WUA_STATE[st],.003));
  if(o==='Walker'&&q36a[1]) wuaState(q36a[1].pk,WUA_STATE.WI,.008);

  // The nominee is locked by this point. Prepare the normal first debate now,
  // rather than waiting until the post-bin-Laden late-game refresh. This keeps
  // the static Daniels fallback from surviving on non-Daniels routes. If the
  // secret Trump assassination route later qualifies, it deliberately
  // overwrites this slot after Q1195.
  wuaDomesticDebate(1196);
}

function wuaSetSuccessTransition(){
  if(WUA.binLaden==='killed'){
    wuaPostBinEffects({1643:.002,1644:.001});
    wuaSet(1195,"The political world has already moved on to the obvious question: how much are we going to use the bin Laden operation in the campaign? Republicans are giving the military and intelligence community most of the credit, which is fair enough, but they're also a lot quieter about calling you weak on national security. The campaign wants a rule before everybody starts freelancing. How hard do you want to lean on this?",[
      {text:"I'm not campaigning on a dead man. If national security comes up, I'll defend the decision and our record, but this election is about what we do next.",fb:"That's restrained, and it keeps us from looking like we're taking a victory lap over somebody's death. The operation still helps every time Republicans try to call you weak."},
      {text:"We don't need to exploit it, but I'm also not going to let Republicans pretend it didn't happen. When they say this administration won't act, remind people that when we found the man who attacked this country, I made the call.",fb:"That's the line the campaign wanted. It isn't especially subtle, but it gives us an answer to one of the attacks Republicans have been using for four years without turning every speech into a bin Laden rally."}
    ]);
  } else if(WUA.binLaden==='captured'){
    wuaPostBinEffects({1643:.002,1644:.001});
    wuaSet(1195,"With bin Laden now in American custody, the campaign is getting the same question everywhere: how much should we make this part of the reelection argument? Republicans are still fighting about how he should be tried, but it's become considerably harder for them to say this administration simply refuses to act against threats. How do you want to handle it?",[
      {text:"Keep it out of the stump speech. We did our job. The trial and the intelligence work aren't campaign props.",fb:"Good. We got him. We don't need to spend the next four months reminding everybody every fifteen minutes. Republicans know the “Kucinich is too weak to protect you” line just got a hell of a lot harder to sell."},
      {text:"Don't turn it into a circus, but when Republicans call us weak, answer them. We found bin Laden and brought him into American custody without starting another war.",fb:"Exactly. We don't need to turn the guy into a campaign mascot, but we're also not going to stand there quietly while Republicans call you a coward. We found bin Laden and brought him back alive. Let them explain how that's weakness."}
    ]);
  }
}

function wuaSetDelayedBinPayoff(){
  WUA.delayedBinOutcome=true;
  if(WUA.binLaden==='killed'){
    wuaPostBinEffects({1643:.006,1644:.012});
    wuaSet(1195,"It's official. The operation is over and Osama bin Laden is dead. Waiting for better intelligence gave the CIA the confidence it needed, and once you authorized the raid the team found him inside the compound. Crowds are already gathering outside the White House and at Ground Zero. For the first time in a long time, Americans seem genuinely united about something. You'll be addressing the country tonight. What do you want to say?",[
      {text:"This should be a somber moment, not a celebration. Osama bin Laden murdered thousands of innocent people, and justice has finally caught up with him. But his death won't bring those people back, and it shouldn't become an excuse for more war. Let's honor the victims, thank the people who carried out this mission and remind Americans that peace is still the goal.",fb:"The speech is restrained and thoughtful. Families of some victims appreciate that you refuse to turn bin Laden's death into a victory parade. A few commentators wonder why you couldn't just let the country celebrate, but the response is positive and nobody can deny who made the call."},
      {text:"Tonight is about justice and closure. Osama bin Laden spent ten years believing he could hide from the United States after murdering thousands of Americans. He was wrong. I want to thank the intelligence officers who found him, the men who carried out this operation and every American who never forgot what happened on September 11th.",fb:"This is the moment the country wanted. Even people who've questioned whether you're tough enough as Commander-in-Chief have a hard time making that case tonight. Your approval jumps, and for once Republicans don't have much of an answer. Enjoy it, Dennis. These days don't come often."}
    ]);
  } else {
    wuaPostBinEffects({1643:.006,1644:.003});
    wuaSet(1195,"Pakistan moved on the compound, and this time the intelligence was right. Osama bin Laden is alive and in custody. Islamabad has agreed to transfer him to the United States after questioning, and now the country is arguing over what happens next. Republicans want a military commission, civil-liberties groups want a civilian trial, and everybody is looking to you. Where do we put him and how do we try him?",[
      {text:"He's a mass murderer, and we already have a justice system for mass murderers. What's more of a vindication of our democratic government than publicly trying a man who sought to destroy it? It'd be a beautiful thing.",fb:"Civil libertarians love it, Republicans absolutely do not. Still, with bin Laden in American custody, the argument that our justice system can handle him is a powerful one."},
      {text:"At the end of the day, he declared war on our country. Give him due process, give him a defense, but try him before a military commission.",fb:"Republicans still find things to complain about, but this is close enough to what many of them wanted that the fight never really catches fire."}
    ]);
  }
}

function wuaApplyQ25(){
  if(wuaRussLeaves()){ if(!WUA.feingoldExit){ WUA.feingoldExit=true; NationalUnity-=.8; ApprovalRating-=.3; } wuaSet(10,"Russ asked to see you alone this morning. He gets right to it: he won't seek renomination as Vice President. He'll serve out the term, endorse you, campaign for the ticket and do whatever he can to help in Wisconsin, but he says the last year has convinced him he can't sign up for another four years in the administration. Republicans are already going to have a field day with this. What do you tell him?",[
    {text:"I respect it, Russ. I wish you were staying, but I'm not going to turn four years of friendship into a public feud because you made a decision I don't like.",fb:"Russ appreciates it. He makes clear publicly that he's leaving on his own terms and still supports your reelection. The story is still bad for us, but at least we minimized the damage."},
    {text:"I need you to reconsider. Whatever disagreements we've had, this ticket works, and walking away now gives Republicans exactly the story they want.",fb:"Russ hears you out, but his mind is made up. He promises to make the announcement as orderly as possible and emphasizes that he'll still campaign for you. It doesn't stop the speculation about why he's leaving, but it keeps the split from getting uglier."},
    {text:"You couldn't have decided this before we filed for reelection? Fine. I'll find somebody else, but don't expect me to pretend this doesn't make the campaign harder.",fb:"That lands badly. Russ doesn't retaliate publicly, but the tension is obvious enough that reporters immediately start digging into every disagreement the two of you have had over the last four years. What could've been a controlled transition now looks a lot more like a breakup."}
  ]); }
}

const WUA_TICKETS = {
  Daniels:{first:"Mitch",last:"Daniels",state:"Indiana",vp:"Marco Rubio"},
  Romney:{first:"Mitt",last:"Romney",state:"Massachusetts",vp:"Paul Ryan"},
  Huntsman:{first:"Jon",last:"Huntsman",state:"Utah",vp:"Nikki Haley"},
  Walker:{first:"Scott",last:"Walker",state:"Wisconsin",vp:"Susan Collins"},
  Christie:{first:"Chris",last:"Christie",state:"New Jersey",vp:"Jack Keane"},
  Trump:{first:"Donald",last:"Trump",state:"New York",vp:"Rob Portman"},
  Cain:{first:"Herman",last:"Cain",state:"Georgia",vp:"Greg Abbott"},
  Brewer:{first:"Jan",last:"Brewer",state:"Arizona",vp:"John Thune"},
  Palin:{first:"Sarah",last:"Palin",state:"Alaska",vp:"Michele Bachmann"},
  West:{first:"Allen",last:"West",state:"Florida",vp:"Jan Brewer"}
};

function wuaSyncOpponentIdentity(){
  const t=WUA_TICKETS[WUA.opponent] || WUA_TICKETS.Daniels;
  const cand=campaignTrail_temp.candidate_json.find(x=>Number(x.pk)===17);
  if(cand && cand.fields){
    cand.fields.first_name=t.first;
    cand.fields.last_name=t.last;
    cand.fields.state=t.state;
  }

  // CTS builds can cache opponent display names separately from candidate_json.
  // Set the common runtime fields too so map/polls/debate UI all stay on the same route.
  campaignTrail_temp.opponent_first_name=t.first;
  campaignTrail_temp.opponent_last_name=t.last;
  campaignTrail_temp.opponent_name=t.first+" "+t.last;
  campaignTrail_temp.opponent_candidate_id=17;
}

function wuaRouteOpponent(){
  // Route from the overall shape of the presidency instead of one-answer guarantees.
  // Romney and Daniels are the conventional baselines; the others need a more
  // distinctive governing record before they can overtake them.
  const A=campaignTrail_temp.player_answers || [];
  const has=x=>A.includes(x);

  const weakEconomy=Math.max(0,1.00-Economy);
  const middlingEconomy=Math.max(0,1.4-Math.abs(Economy-1.00));
  const stableUnity=Math.max(0,1.3-Math.abs(NationalUnity-.4));
  const lowApproval=Math.max(0,49-ApprovalRating)/2;
  const highApproval=Math.max(0,ApprovalRating-50)/3;

  // The BP fight is the main environmental-policy signal in this scenario.
  // Going hardest at BP can strengthen the business/populist backlash routes,
  // but it never determines the nominee on its own.
  const greenBacklash=has(1703)?1.2:has(1702)?.45:0;

  const scores={
    Romney:4.35 + 1.35*weakEconomy + .15*lowApproval,
    Daniels:(SocialPolicy>=-1 && SocialPolicy<=4)
      ? 4.30 + .65*middlingEconomy + .45*stableUnity + .15*highApproval
      : -999,

    Huntsman:(NationalUnity>0.90 && Economy>1.00 && ApprovalRating>48)
      ? 3.65 + .65*(NationalUnity-0.90) + .40*(Economy-1.00) + .18*(ApprovalRating-48)
      : -999,

    // Wisconsin/labor makes Walker possible, not automatic. He still needs an
    // underwhelming economy for the anti-union economic argument to catch fire.
    Walker:(Economy<2.65 && (WUA.laborPush || has(343)))
      ? 3.25 + (WUA.laborPush?1.20:0) + (has(343)?1.25:0)
           + .32*(2.65-Economy) + .15*Math.max(0,-NationalUnity)
      : -999,

    Christie:(ForeignPolicy>2.1 && NationalUnity>-3.7)
      ? 3.90 + .70*(ForeignPolicy-2.1) + .15*Math.max(0,-NationalUnity)
      : -999,

    Trump:(NationalUnity<=-8 && (Economy<2.35 || ApprovalRating<52))
      ? 3.65 + .35*(0.15-NationalUnity) + .40*greenBacklash + .28*weakEconomy + .10*lowApproval
          + (WUA.executivePower?.45:0) + (has(181)?.3:0)
          + .15*Math.max(0,SocialPolicy-2)
      : -999,

    Cain:(ApprovalRating<50.5 && Economy>.65)
      ? 3.00 + .65*(50.5-ApprovalRating)/2 + .85*greenBacklash
          + .2*Math.max(0,Economy-1) + (has(181)?.2:0)
      : -999,

    Brewer:-999,
    Palin:-999,
    West:-999
  };

  // Border politics can elevate Brewer, but no single Arizona answer guarantees her.
  const borderHeat=has(83)?1.0:has(81)?.35:0;
  if(borderHeat && NationalUnity<1.45){
    scores.Brewer=3.75 + borderHeat + .22*Math.max(0,-NationalUnity)
      + .15*Math.max(0,SocialPolicy-1.5);
  }

  // Palin is the broad ideological-backlash route: sustained polarization plus
  // a distinctly progressive social-policy record.
  if(NationalUnity<=-6 && SocialPolicy>1.0){
    scores.Palin=3.75 + .30*(-NationalUnity-1.1) + .43*(SocialPolicy-1.0)
      + (WUA.executivePower?.35:0) + (has(1690)?.3:0);
  }

  // West requires the most combustible combination: deep division, a strongly
  // dovish foreign-policy record, low approval, and either executive-power
  // confrontation or an exceptionally fractured country.
  if(NationalUnity<-1.05 && ForeignPolicy>2.1 && ApprovalRating<54
     && (WUA.executivePower || NationalUnity<-2.0)){
    scores.West=3.65 + .28*(-NationalUnity-1.05) + .42*(ForeignPolicy-2.1)
       + .22*(54-ApprovalRating)/2 + (WUA.executivePower?.55:0);
  }

  WUA.opponent=Object.keys(scores).reduce((best,k)=>scores[k]>scores[best]?k:best,'Daniels');
  wuaSyncOpponentIdentity();
}
const WUA_REVEALS={
Daniels:"Well, Republicans didn't give us the lunatic we were hoping for. Mitch Daniels survived the primaries by selling himself as the boring policy wonk in the room, and apparently Republican voters decided boring doesn't sound so bad after four years of us. He's picked Florida Senator Marco Rubio as his running mate, which gives the ticket some youthful energy. They're already making the economy and federal spending the centerpiece of their campaign. They're a formidable ticket, but they're not invincible. We need a strategy.",
Romney:"Well, Republicans have decided not to get too crazy. Mitt Romney is their nominee, and he's picked Wisconsin Congressman Paul Ryan as his running mate. They're making this election a straight referendum on the economy: four years, trillions spent, and too many people still struggling. Romney gives them the businessman, Ryan gives them the budget hawk. Where do we start?",
Huntsman:"Well, Jon Huntsman came out of nowhere to win the nomination, selling Republicans on the idea that they don't need to burn Washington down to beat us. He's picked South Carolina Governor Nikki Haley as his running mate, giving him a younger, more conservative partner who should help with the parts of the party that still aren't completely sold on him. Huntsman's already making a big deal out of his record as Governor of Utah and trying to position himself as the reasonable alternative to four more years of us. He's harder to paint as some right-wing lunatic because, frankly, he isn't one. What's our reaction?",
Walker:"Well, I hope you enjoyed those trips to Wisconsin, because Scott Walker certainly did. Republicans have nominated him for President, and he's spent the entire primary turning his fight with the unions into a national argument about whether government works for taxpayers or the people who work for it. He's picked Maine Senator Susan Collins as his running mate, which gives the ticket a considerably more moderate face and makes it harder to write them off as some Tea Party crusade. Walker's already making this personal, saying you came into his state to defend ‘union bosses’ and now he intends to take their agenda apart nationally. Russ looks like he wants to punch the television. How do we respond?",
Christie:"Well, Jack Keane finally decided he'd rather run against us than keep going on Fox to complain. Republicans have nominated New Jersey Governor Chris Christie for President, and he's picked retired General Jack Keane as his running mate. You know Keane's argument by now: we pulled out of Afghanistan too quickly, we've sent mixed signals from Egypt to Libya to Syria, and this administration has spent four years apologizing for America. Christie is wrapping that into his no-nonsense, tough-guy persona. After everything Keane's thrown at us, we've got plenty to respond to. Where do we start?",
Trump:"Well, it actually happened. Donald fucking Trump is the Republican nominee for President. After spending the last two years going on television to call you everything from a socialist to a fascist, Republican voters apparently decided they want more of it. He's picked Ohio Senator Rob Portman as his running mate, presumably so somebody on the ticket knows how basic government works. Trump is promising to ‘take our country back,’ slap tariffs on China, tear up our healthcare law and potentially jail you. He's also already making sick jokes about Elizabeth. This is going to be a long campaign. Where the hell do we start?",
Cain:"Well, apparently Republicans decided they needed to amp up the crazy. Herman Cain is their nominee, and he's picked Texas Attorney General Greg Abbott as his running mate. Cain's selling himself as the businessman who's going to bring some common sense to Washington, with that damn 9-9-9 tax plan. Abbott brings some experience, as he's spent the last four years fighting just about everything we've tried to do in federal court. Cain's charismatic, and after four years of us, his outsider act is connecting with people. Where do we start?",
Brewer:"Well, remember when we thought Jan Brewer was just going to be a problem in Arizona? Republicans have nominated her for President. She's spent the primaries turning the fight over immigration into a broader argument that this administration is soft on crime, and apparently Republican voters liked what they heard. She's picked South Dakota Senator John Thune as her running mate, giving the ticket somebody with more experience in Washington. Brewer's already bringing up our confrontation over Arizona every chance she gets. Where do we start?",
Palin:"I don't even know where to start with this one. Republicans have nominated Sarah Palin for President, and she's picked Michele Bachmann as her running mate. Apparently four years of calling you a socialist wasn't enough, because Republican primary voters have decided they want to make the entire election about taking the country back from the radical left. Palin says this administration has weakened America abroad, destroyed the economy at home and spent four years trampling on the Constitution. Bachmann, somehow, is even worse. Democrats are practically celebrating, but we've learned not to underestimate these kinds of people. Where do we begin?",
West:"Jesus Christ. Republicans have nominated Florida Congressman Allen West for President, and he's picked Jan Brewer as his running mate. West spent the primaries calling this administration socialist, Marxist and just about every other word he can think of. Now he's claiming that dozens of Democrats in Congress are literally members of the Communist Party. This guy is like a modern day Joe McCarthy with better hair. Brewer gives him experience on the ticket and they've already made immigration, crime and national security the center of their campaign. How do we respond to this?"
};
function wuaApplyReveal(pk){
  if(Number(pk)===1190) WUA.oppositionUnlocked=true;
  wuaSyncOpponentIdentity();
  let o=WUA.opponent; let answers;
  if(o==='Romney') answers=[
    {text:"Start with Bain. Romney made a fortune restructuring companies while workers lost jobs. If he wants to make this about business experience, let's talk about what that experience actually meant."},
    {text:"Paul Ryan wants to turn Medicare into a voucher system. Put that front and center. Seniors need to know exactly what this ticket wants to do."},
    {text:"Romney already lost this argument once in 2008, and he's reinvented himself about six times since then. Make this about whether anybody knows what he actually believes."}
  ];
  else if(o==='Huntsman') answers=[
    {text:"He seems reasonable, but he's still a Republican running on Republican economics. Tax cuts at the top, Republican judges, the whole thing."},
    {text:"Ask whether he's actually willing to stand up to the people in his own party who've spent four years trying to tear down everything we've done."},
    {text:"Don't call him something he isn't. He's not a lunatic. Run on our record and let the differences speak for themselves."}
  ];
  else if(o==='Walker') answers=[
    {text:"Then let's have the labor fight nationally. I went to Wisconsin because what he did was wrong, and I'm not backing away from it now.",fb:"Walker is perfectly happy to have this fight. He points to surviving the recall and says Wisconsin voters already rejected our argument once. Labor loves the aggression, but this is going to be one of the central battles of the campaign."},
    {text:"He promised Wisconsin 250,000 private-sector jobs. Make him defend the whole record, not just the fight with the unions.",fb:"Walker keeps trying to steer it back to the unions. Don't let him. Every time he does, bring it right back to those 250,000 jobs he promised Wisconsin. He made the number up, not us."},
    {text:"Susan Collins is supposed to make this ticket look moderate? Let's start asking which Scott Walker positions she actually agrees with.",fb:"Collins immediately becomes the focus of a lot of uncomfortable questions about where she agrees with Walker and where she doesn't. It doesn't undermine Walker himself much, but it complicates the moderate image the ticket was hoping she'd provide."}
  ];
  else if(o==='Christie') answers=[
    {text:"Jack Keane never met a war he didn't want to escalate. Start with Afghanistan and make them explain exactly how many more years they wanted us there."},
    {text:"Christie picked a general as his running mate and suddenly we're supposed to pretend that gives him foreign-policy experience? He's still a governor from New Jersey."},
    {text:"Pull every clip of Keane demanding more troops, more escalation and more intervention. If they want to run as the hawkish ticket, make them own it."}
  ];
  else if(o==='Trump') answers=[
    {text:"Bankruptcies, the reality show, every ridiculous thing he's said. Make the case that this guy is completely unfit for the office."},
    {text:"Forget the celebrity crap. Talk about the workers and contractors who've gotten screwed by his businesses. That's the Trump story I want people hearing."},
    {text:"Wasn't he pro-choice and supportive of gay couples getting the same legal benefits not that long ago? Let's remind Republican voters who they just nominated."}
  ];
  else if(o==='Cain') answers=[
    {text:"Let's start with 9-9-9. Herman Cain wants to slap a new national sales tax on every family in America while cutting taxes for people at the top. That's a regressive tax that punishes consumers, and we need to emphasize it."},
    {text:"He's never held public office and he doesn't know the first thing about foreign policy. If he wants to run as the outsider, make him explain why that qualifies him to be President."},
    {text:"Abbott's spent four years suing this administration. Fine. Let's ask whether their entire agenda is just repealing everything we've done."}
  ];
  else if(o==='Brewer') answers=[
    {text:"Defend the record. We sent resources to the border and took enforcement seriously. She doesn't get to pretend Washington ignored Arizona."},
    {text:"She's turned one issue into an entire presidential campaign. Make her show voters she has something to say about the economy, healthcare and foreign policy."},
    {text:"Get reporters asking Thune how a national version of SB 1070 is supposed to get through the Senate. He's the one on the ticket who actually knows Congress."}
  ];
  else if(o==='Palin') answers=[
    {text:"Are you kidding me? Just put the two of them on television. Half the things they've said over the last four years sound like something we'd make up in an attack ad. We can focus on our agenda while they make fools of themselves."},
    {text:"I don't think we need to make any personal attacks, let's just stick to the issues. These aren't mainstream Republicans, they're Tea Party wackos. Maybe we reach out to some more moderate Republicans, see if we can't secure an endorsement or two."}
  ];
  else if(o==='West') answers=[
    {text:"These guys call everything communism! Maybe he can explain exactly what communist policy I've enacted over the last four years."},
    {text:"Forget the communist crap. Compare our leadership and records. If he wants to make this about who's fit to be President, I'm happy to do that."}
  ];
  else answers=[
    {text:"Well for starters, as Governor of Indiana Daniels privatized the department of corrections. Let's talk about what that actually did."},
    {text:"He's a typical union-busting Republican. I don't see how we can't hammer him on this, especially with Rubio standing next to him."},
    {text:"Mitch Daniels wants to lecture this administration about deficits? He ran George W. Bush's budget office. Let's remind people what happened to the surplus."}
  ];

  const revealFb={
    Daniels:[
      "Daniels has a real record to defend in Indiana, and labor groups are happy to help us make sure voters hear about it. Republicans answer that his reelection proves Hoosiers already heard these attacks and weren't impressed.",
      "Labor loves it, but Daniels has spent years presenting himself as a pragmatic and inoffensive choice. It's not that we don't have an argument, he's just harder to portray as ideological.",
      "That's probably our strongest opening contrast. Daniels can't completely escape his Bush budget-office years, even if he insists the administration inherited most of the fiscal mess."
    ],
    Romney:[
      "Bain is an obvious target and Democrats immediately start lining up workers willing to tell their stories. Romney has heard this attack before, though, and his campaign is ready for it.",
      "Ryan gives us a very clean policy contrast. Republicans are happy to defend his budget, but that means they spend the opening days talking about Medicare and spending cuts instead of just the unemployment rate.",
      "This gets directly at the argument Romney wants to make about being a competent manager. The problem for him is that every business success story comes with another debate over who actually benefited."
    ],
    Huntsman:[
      "It's a fair contrast, although Huntsman's moderate reputation makes the usual Republican-label attacks less effective than we'd like.",
      "This puts Huntsman in an awkward spot. Every time he distances himself from the Republican Congress he risks irritating his own base; every time he embraces them he weakens the image he's selling.",
      "Probably the smartest approach. Calling Huntsman a lunatic would just make us look ridiculous. The differences are real enough without inventing new ones."
    ],
    Walker:[
      "Walker is perfectly happy to have this fight. He points to surviving the recall and says Wisconsin voters already rejected our argument once. Labor loves the aggression, but this is going to be one of the central battles of the campaign.",
      "This is a better opening. Walker wants the campaign to be about standing up to public-sector unions; forcing him to defend the broader economic record gives us more ground to work with.",
      "Collins immediately gets uncomfortable questions about where she agrees with Walker and where she doesn't. It complicates the moderate image the ticket wanted, even if it doesn't hurt Walker directly."
    ],
    Christie:[
      "Keane has a long paper trail arguing for escalation, so this gives us plenty to work with. Christie answers that withdrawing too quickly carries costs too.",
      "We really stepped in it with this one. Chris may not have military experience, but as U.S. Attorney he helped prosecute terrorism cases and dealt with national security firsthand. Christie isn't exactly top brass, but he's happy to remind us of his record.",
      "This makes the contrast unmistakable. The downside is that Christie and Keane are completely comfortable running as the more hawkish ticket."
    ],
    Trump:[
      "There's certainly no shortage of material. The danger is obvious too: every personal attack turns into another day of Donald Trump dominating the news.",
      "This is stronger ground. Contractors and workers give the business-genius image some ugly complications, and Trump has a harder time dismissing them as just politicians.",
      "Conservative activists notice immediately. Trump claims he evolved and starts attacking us instead, but at least part of the Republican coalition spends a day arguing with itself."
    ],
    Cain:[
      "This one lands pretty well. Cain is more than happy to talk about 9-9-9, but the more people hear “national sales tax,” the less catchy the name starts sounding. He gets plenty of airtime to sell the plan, but so do we.",
      "Cain's lack of experience is definitely a vulnerability. Still, attacking him for being an outsider has limited effect when that's a big part of why people like him in the first place. Plus, it wasn't long ago you were seen as a wild outsider, too.",
      "Abbott is happy to fight us on the lawsuits. The attack works better as a reminder that the ticket's governing agenda is mostly repeal and confrontation than as a personal hit on Cain."
    ],
    Brewer:[
      "Brewer says Arizona acted because Washington failed. We're at least fighting on the facts now, but this is exactly the subject she wanted the opening week to be about.",
      "This broadens the campaign in a useful way. Brewer can dominate an immigration argument; she has a much harder time making every other presidential issue disappear.",
      "Thune gets stuck answering for policies he didn't write. It doesn't break the ticket, but it makes the national version of Brewer's agenda look more complicated than her rallies suggest."
    ],
    Palin:[
      "There's plenty of footage, and the ticket produces more almost every day. The risk is looking smug while Palin turns every attack into proof that elites hate her voters.",
      "Moderate Republicans are receptive, and a few start making noises about sitting the race out. Palin's people immediately use that as evidence that the establishment is terrified of her."
    ],
    West:[
      "West doesn't back away from any of it. He says the language is harsh because the threat is real, which thrills his supporters and alarms just about everyone else.",
      "This is the more disciplined contrast. West wants a screaming match about communism; forcing the discussion back onto experience and judgment makes that harder for him."
    ]
  };
  const rfb=revealFb[o]||revealFb.Daniels;
  answers.forEach((a,i)=>{ if(!a.fb) a.fb=rfb[Math.min(i,rfb.length-1)]; });

  wuaSet(pk,WUA_REVEALS[o]||WUA_REVEALS.Daniels,answers); wuaApplyTicketMap(pk);
  if(Number(pk)===1190 && o==='Huntsman'){
    wuaAnswers(pk).forEach(a=>{
      let score=campaignTrail_temp.answer_score_global_json.find(r=>Number(r.fields.answer)===Number(a.pk) && Number(r.fields.affected_candidate)===19);
      if(!score){
        score={model:'campaign_trail.answer_score_global',pk:991900+Number(a.pk),fields:{answer:Number(a.pk),candidate:16,affected_candidate:19,global_multiplier:0.0776}};
        campaignTrail_temp.answer_score_global_json.push(score);
      } else score.fields.global_multiplier=0.0776;
    });
  }
  if(Number(pk)===1190) wuaPrepareOpponentCampaign();
}
function wuaDomesticDebate(pk){
 const o=WUA.opponent;
 if(o==='Romney') wuaSet(pk,"It's the first presidential debate. Romney's been sharp tonight, and now he's landed his strongest attack. ‘President Kucinich inherited a terrible economy. Nobody disputes that. But after four years, millions of Americans are still struggling, and at some point a President has to stop telling people what he inherited and start answering for what he did with it.’ The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?",[
  {text:"He's right about one thing: after four years, this is my economy too. We haven't fixed everything, but unemployment is down, the auto industry is alive, millions more people have healthcare and we're moving in the right direction. I'm not going to tell Americans everything is wonderful, but I'm damn sure not going to pretend we're where we were four years ago.",fb:"This is probably your best moment of the night. You acknowledge what people can plainly see without accepting Romney's argument that the last four years were a failure. Even some Republicans concede afterward that it was a strong answer."},
  {text:"Governor Romney's very good at telling Americans they're struggling. What he hasn't explained is how another round of tax cuts for people at the top and an unregulated Wall Street is supposed to help them. We tried that experiment already. I'm not interested in doing it again.",fb:"Romney immediately says you're attacking a caricature of his plan and spends the next several minutes explaining his tax proposal. We don't win every point, but we've moved the conversation away from whether you're responsible for the recovery and onto what Romney would actually do."},
  {text:"Mitt Romney wants me to take responsibility for the economy? Fine. Maybe he can take some responsibility for the Republican policies that crashed it in the first place. Tax cuts for the wealthy, deregulation and Wall Street running wild got us into this mess. I'm not taking lectures from somebody who wants to go right back to it.",fb:"Romney was waiting for this. ‘There he goes again. Four years later, and it's still George Bush's fault.’ It's one of his biggest applause lines of the night. This was a mistake."}
 ]);
 else if(o==='Huntsman') wuaSet(pk,"It's the first presidential debate. Huntsman's having a good night. When you criticize Republican obstruction, he responds that you've spent four years blaming everyone else for Washington's dysfunction. ‘Mr. President, you can't spend four years telling Americans you're the man who can bring people together and then blame Congress every time you don't get your way.’ Even some Democrats admit that landed. What's your response?",[
  {text:"Jon, we've worked with Republicans plenty of times. We worked together on the first stimulus, border security and plenty of other things nobody remembers because cooperation doesn't make very exciting television. I've compromised when I thought it would help the country. I'm not going to compromise just for the sake of saying I did it.",fb:"Huntsman acknowledges that you've worked with Republicans and says that's exactly the kind of governing he'd like to see more of. It's a remarkably civil exchange for a presidential debate, and independents respond well to it. This was a good answer."},
  {text:"That's easy to say when you weren't there, Jon. I sat across the table from Republican leaders who told us no before they knew our proposal. I can say no to balancing our budget on the backs of working people and still work together when needed. That's what real leadership is.",fb:"Huntsman calmly reminds you that he's spent years criticizing his own party for exactly that kind of behavior. Attacking Republican obstructionism is good, but Huntsman isn't tying himself to Republican leadership."},
  {text:"Jon Huntsman talks a lot about moderation, but there's nothing moderate about eliminating taxes on capital gains and dividends while cutting taxes at the top. We can disagree politely, but let's not pretend the choice here isn't real.",fb:"Huntsman defends his plan as pro-growth tax reform, but now we're arguing about a policy difference instead of character. His moderate image is nice, but it doesn't fully cover the old ideas he has."}
 ]);
 else if(o==='Walker') wuaSet(pk,"It's the first presidential debate. Walker finally gets the opening he's been waiting for. ‘The President came into Wisconsin and tried to tell our voters how to run their own state. We balanced our budget, stood up to the special interests, and when his allies tried to throw me out of office, the people of Wisconsin reelected me.’ He turns directly toward you. ‘Mr. President, Wisconsin already voted on your agenda once. You lost.’ That's a hell of a line. How do you answer it?",[
  {text:"I'd do it again. Public employees had their collective bargaining rights stripped away, and I wasn't going to sit quietly in Washington while it happened. You won the recall, Scott. Congratulations. That doesn't mean you were right.",fb:"Walker fires right back that this is exactly his point: even after you and Russ came into Wisconsin personally, voters rejected your argument. Our people love that you don't back down, but we're fighting the debate on Walker's terms."},
  {text:"You didn't win the national election. You had a recall election about whether the Governor of Wisconsin should be removed halfway through his term. You won it. Now we're having an election about who should be President of the United States. Maybe we should talk about that.",fb:"He argues that the recall was obviously about more than Wisconsin, but this ends up making him seem petty and too focused on one election when the country needs solutions. Good call."}
 ]);
 else if(o==='Christie') wuaSet(pk,"It's the first presidential debate. Christie's been hammering the economy all night, but now he turns to governing. ‘This President always has somebody else to blame. Republicans, bankers, corporations, governors, anybody except the guy sitting in the Oval Office. Leadership means getting something done with people you don't like. I've governed New Jersey with a Democratic legislature and somehow we still managed to get things done.’ He turns toward you. ‘Maybe the problem in Washington isn't everybody else, Mr. President.’ The audience likes that one. How do you respond?",[
  {text:"We've gotten plenty done, Chris. We passed healthcare reform, saved the auto industry, invested in this economy and worked with Republicans when they were willing to work with us. I'm happy to take responsibility for my record. I just won't take responsibility for theirs.",fb:"Christie says that's exactly the kind of answer he's talking about, but we've got a record to defend. Reminding people of our accomplishments is always a positive."},
  {text:"Governor, your idea of bipartisan compromise was getting Democrats to help you cut public employees' benefits. I'm sure Republicans in Congress would be happy to work with me too if I agreed to everything they wanted. That's not compromise.",fb:"Christie was ready for this. He points out that New Jersey's pension reform really did pass with Democratic legislative support and says you're insulting Democrats who crossed party lines to solve a problem. We walked straight into that one."},
  {text:"There's something I agree with you about, Chris. Presidents have to work with people they disagree with. I've done that, and sometimes I've angered my own party doing it. But compromise has to produce something that's actually good for the country. Otherwise, you're just making a deal so you can say you made one.",fb:"Christie says he'd agree with almost every word of that. For a minute this turns into an actual discussion about what compromise means instead of another shouting match. Neither of you gets much of a hit in, but you look perfectly comfortable standing next to him."}
 ]);
 else if(o==='Trump') wuaSet(pk,"It's the first presidential debate. This debate has completely gone off the rails. Trump has interrupted you so many times the moderator has stopped trying to count. He's called the healthcare law ‘Kucinichcare,’ referred to you twice as ‘Dennis the Menace,’ and just finished a three-minute rant about how you're ‘probably the worst negotiator this country has ever had.’ The moderator finally gets control of the room and asks you about the federal deficit. Trump interrupts again before you've finished your first sentence. What the hell do you do?",[
  {text:"Donald, would you shut the hell up for thirty seconds and let me answer the question? You'll get your turn.",fb:"Well, that gets the biggest reaction of the night. Trump looks almost delighted and immediately starts shouting back while the moderator desperately tries to regain control. Your supporters love seeing you stand up to him, but whatever debate we were supposed to be having is officially over."},
  {text:"I'm sorry, I know I'm supposed to be answering a question about the deficit, but apparently Donald has something very important to tell us. Go ahead, Donald. Finish whatever it is you're trying to say.",fb:"Trump happily takes the invitation and rambles for another minute while you stand there smiling. Eventually even the moderator stops interrupting him. The audience starts laughing, and Trump gets noticeably angrier when he realizes some of them are laughing at him."},
  {text:"I'm going to answer the moderator's question. We've put forward proposals to reduce the deficit without balancing the budget on the backs of working people or gutting Social Security and Medicare. Donald can interrupt me as many times as he wants. I'm still going to answer it.",fb:"Trump keeps trying to interrupt, but you simply talk through him. It isn't as entertaining as the shouting match everybody expected, but after an hour of chaos, looking like the only person on the stage interested in actually discussing policy isn't a bad contrast."}
 ]);
 else if(o==='Cain') wuaSet(pk,"It's the first presidential debate. Well, here it comes. The moderator asks Cain directly about the sexual harassment allegations from his time at the National Restaurant Association and whether voters should consider them when judging his character. Cain denies the allegations completely, calls the whole thing a political smear and then turns toward you. ‘Maybe President Kucinich would like to tell us whether he thinks these women are telling the truth. If he wants to question my character, he should have the courage to do it himself.’ The moderator asks if you'd like to respond. Do you?",[
  {text:"I don't know exactly what happened, Herman, and neither does anybody watching this debate. These are serious allegations, and the women making them deserve to be heard, but I'm not going to stand here and declare somebody guilty when I don't know the facts.",fb:"Cain clearly wanted a fight and doesn't get one. He repeats that the allegations are false, but there's not much for him to attack you over. The moderator moves on while the questions about Cain remain unanswered."},
  {text:"There were complaints, there were settlements and Herman has given different answers about what happened. I'm not going to tell voters what conclusion they should reach, but I think they're entitled to an honest explanation from somebody asking to be President.",fb:"Cain angrily accuses you of repeating unproven allegations and says you're participating in the same smear campaign he's been fighting for months. This was ugly, but it's hard to argue Cain won this one."}
 ]);
 else if(o==='Brewer') wuaSet(pk,"It's the first presidential debate. Brewer's been waiting all night for immigration to come up, and the moderator finally gives her the opening. ‘When I was Governor of Arizona, I begged Washington to secure our border. President Kucinich had plenty of criticism for what Arizona did, but criticism doesn't stop drug smugglers and human traffickers from crossing the border.’ She turns toward you. ‘Mr. President, if Arizona was so wrong, what exactly were we supposed to do while Washington failed to do its job?’ How do you answer?",[
  {text:"Jan, we did send resources to the border. What I wouldn't do was pretend that every undocumented immigrant in Arizona was a threat to public safety. We can enforce our laws and secure the border without turning millions of people into political scapegoats.",fb:"Brewer says that's exactly the kind of Washington lecture that forced Arizona to act in the first place. Still, we've got an actual border-security record to point to, and that makes it harder for her to claim we simply ignored the problem."},
  {text:"You weren't wrong to demand help from Washington. Where we disagreed was SB 1070. Immigration is a federal responsibility, and having fifty states writing fifty different immigration policies isn't going to solve anything. We needed better enforcement and comprehensive reform, and that's what I've tried to deliver.",fb:"That's a pretty reasonable distinction. Brewer keeps defending SB 1070, but you've conceded the part of her argument that most voters sympathize with without acting like her solution made sense."}
 ]);
 else if(o==='Palin') wuaSet(pk,"It's the first presidential debate. Well, Palin came prepared. She's avoided most of the mistakes Democrats were hoping for, and now she lands one of her better lines of the night. ‘Dennis Kucinich came to Washington promising change. Four years later, Washington spends more, owes more and argues more. Maybe the problem isn't that President Kucinich needs four more years. Maybe four years was already too many.’ There's a big reaction from the audience, and Palin looks pretty pleased with herself. How do you respond?",[
  {text:"Sarah, four years ago this country was losing hundreds of thousands of jobs a month, the financial system was collapsing and the auto industry was on the edge of bankruptcy. If you want to ask whether Americans are better off than they were then, I'm perfectly happy to have that argument.",fb:"Palin says the question isn't whether things are better than at the bottom of a historic financial crisis, but whether they're as good as they should be after four years. That's a fair comeback, but reminding voters exactly what we inherited still works."},
  {text:"That's a good line, Sarah. But being President isn't about having the best line. You actually have to make decisions. We've passed healthcare reform, saved the auto industry and gotten this country moving again. If you want this job, tell Americans what you'd actually do differently.",fb:"Palin doesn't stumble. She runs through tax cuts, spending reductions, deregulation and ‘drill baby drill’. We were hoping she'd fall apart when pressed for specifics. She doesn't. It's not a massive win for her, but it's not a win for us."},
  {text:"You know what? That's pretty good. Did Michele write that one for you?",fb:"Oh, for God's sake. Palin smiles and says, ‘No, Dennis, I can actually write my own speeches.’ The audience loves it. We spent months assuming she'd embarrass herself on a debate stage and then handed her the easiest applause line of the night."}
 ]);
 else if(o==='West') wuaSet(pk,"It's the first presidential debate. West's been itching for a fight all night, and he finally gets one. ‘The problem with this President isn't complicated. It's weakness. Our enemies don't respect him, criminals don't fear him and Congress doesn't listen to him. America doesn't need a community organizer for the radical left. We need a Commander-in-Chief.’ He reminds the audience that he spent more than twenty years in the Army and then turns directly toward you. ‘Mr. President, what exactly have you ever commanded?’ The crowd erupts. How do you answer?",[
  {text:"Allen, I respect your service to this country. But being President isn't about proving you're the toughest man in the room. It's about knowing when to use American power and having the judgment not to use it when you don't have to. That's what a Commander-in-Chief does.",fb:"West says that's exactly the kind of weakness he's talking about, but you don't take his bait. He can talk about toughness all he wants; you've spent four years actually making these decisions. This comes across as presidential."},
  {text:"You want to talk about your military record? Fine. You threatened to kill an Iraqi detainee and fired a gun near his head during an interrogation. The Army disciplined you for it. That's not the kind of judgment I want anywhere near the Oval Office.",fb:"West was practically begging for this. He says he did what he had to do to protect American soldiers and that he'd make the same decision again. His supporters go wild, but now the entire country is hearing about an incident most voters had never heard of. This gets ugly, but it's not necessarily bad for us."},
  {text:"If Allen West thinks leadership means shouting louder than everybody else and calling anyone who disagrees with him a communist, then I think he's made my argument for me tonight. I've been President for four years. I don't need to pound my chest to prove I can do the job.",fb:"West immediately starts shouting over you about socialism, national security and the Constitution. You mostly stand there and let him go. By the time the moderator gets control back, West has done a pretty good job demonstrating exactly what you were talking about."}
 ]);
}
function wuaVPDebate(pk){
 const n=wuaVPName(), him=wuaVPPoss(), o=WUA.opponent;
 if(o==='Daniels') wuaSet(pk,`Rubio's preparing to make the age of this administration an issue without actually saying the word ‘old.’ His argument is that Daniels and Rubio represent a new generation of leadership while we've spent four years defending the same Democratic ideas Washington's been arguing about for decades. ${n} wants to know how you want ${him} to handle it if Rubio tries this line. What do you suggest?`,[
  {text:"Turn it around on him. Tell Rubio there's nothing new about tax cuts for the wealthy, deregulation and cutting government programs. Republicans have been selling that agenda my entire adult life. A younger salesman doesn't make it a new idea.",fb:`That's a good line, and ${n} delivers it well. Rubio argues that his party has learned from its mistakes, but it's difficult for him to sell the ticket as a completely new direction while defending ideas voters have heard from Republicans for decades.`},
  {text:"Don't get dragged into an argument about who's younger or newer. Make it about experience. We've spent four years governing through a financial crisis, healthcare reform and wars overseas. Rubio's been in the Senate for less than two years. There's a difference between talking about leadership and actually doing it.",fb:`Rubio was ready for this. He reminds the audience that Washington experience isn't exactly popular right now and says four years in power only matters if voters like the results. We score some points, but Rubio gets an opening to run against Washington.`},
  {text:"Turn the whole argument around. Mitch Daniels wants more deregulation and fewer environmental protections, and it's young people who are going to live with the consequences of that. Their ticket can have a youthful face all it wants. There's nothing forward-looking about leaving the next generation a dirtier, more dangerous planet. If Rubio wants to talk about the future, make him defend the policies.",fb:`Rubio smiles and says, “I didn't realize recycling the same liberal talking points for forty years counted as a plan for the future.” That gets a big laugh, and ${n} never quite finds a comeback. We had a serious point to make, but Rubio made us look a little out of touch while we were making it.`}
 ]);
 else if(o==='Romney') wuaSet(pk,`Ryan's been defending his budget all night, and Medicare finally takes center stage. He argues that the program is on an unsustainable path and accuses our administration of refusing to make the difficult choices necessary to preserve it for future generations. ${n} is preparing for the obvious exchange over Ryan's plan and wants to know how hard you want ${him} to go. What do you suggest?`,[
  {text:"Go right at him. Tell Ryan he can dress it up however he wants, but his plan shifts more of the risk onto seniors while giving wealthy Americans another tax cut. We don't save Medicare by asking the people who depend on it to pick up the tab.",fb:`${n} hammers Ryan on Medicare, and Ryan fires back that we're frightening seniors rather than addressing the program's finances. It's an argument Ryan knows well, but it's also one Democrats are very happy to have. We come out ahead.`},
  {text:"Don't exaggerate his plan. Acknowledge that Medicare has long-term problems, then explain how we're going to strengthen it without shifting costs onto seniors. If Ryan wants to have a serious argument about the numbers, let's have one.",fb:`Ryan was expecting you to scream about throwing Grandma off Medicare. Instead, ${n} actually argues the numbers with him. He still gets his shots in, but we come out of this looking a lot more reasonable than he wanted.`},
  WUA.vp==='Patrick'
    ? {text:"Let Deval speak to his own record in Massachusetts, he doesn't really need me getting in his head about anything.",fb:`Patrick handles himself just fine. Ryan attacks his record on crime, but Deval reminds him that the murder rate he inherited was already at its highest level in years under Mitt Romney. Ryan points out that overall violent crime fell under Romney, but the attack has lost most of its punch. Not bad, Deval.`}
    : {text:"Ask Ryan why we're having this conversation at all. The richest country on Earth can afford healthcare for its seniors. We don't have a Medicare crisis; we have a political class that's spent thirty years refusing to ask wealthy people and corporations to pay their fair share.",fb:`Ryan pounces on the claim that there isn't a Medicare problem and starts quoting projections about the program's finances. ${n} gets the argument back onto taxes eventually, but we've handed Ryan an easy way to make himself look like the serious one.`}
 ]);
 else if(o==='Huntsman') wuaSet(pk,`Haley's been going after our healthcare reforms tonight, arguing that we've given Washington too much control over people's medical decisions and that a Huntsman administration would repeal them. She frames it as an issue of personal liberty and freedom of medical choice, arguing that patients should make these decisions instead of Washington. The moderator finally asks what Republicans would replace our reforms with, and Haley starts talking about competition, state flexibility and giving individuals more control over their healthcare. ${n} wants to know where you want ${him} to take the argument. What do you suggest?`,[
  {text:"Ask Nikki what happens the morning after they repeal our reforms. What happens to somebody with a preexisting condition? What happens to a family that finally has coverage? If Huntsman and Haley want to tear this apart, make them tell those people what they're replacing it with. She wants to talk about medical choice, then make sure the people can make an informed choice.",fb:`Haley says Republicans aren't proposing to take healthcare away from anybody and starts laying out their plans for competition and state-level reforms. ${n} keeps bringing her back to the same question: what happens to the people who already have coverage? She never really gives a clear answer. This is exactly the way to hammer them on this.`},
  {text:"And what exactly is this liberty she's talking about? The liberty to die because your insurance provider won't cover a life-saving operation? What kind of choice is that really?",fb:`Haley asks if that's how we see it, why don't we have Medicare-for-All like we wanted, instead of the public option people still pay for. She's got a point, and she made it very well.`},
  {text:"So let's make this simple: what's more radical, a country where millions more Americans have access to healthcare, or taking that healthcare away while funding war? I want that question to be the center of this debate.",fb:`Haley calls that fearmongering and suggests the Huntsman/Haley plan will cover everyone ours does and more. The moderator conveniently moves on before she can be pressed on specifics. Chalk that up as a draw at best.`}
 ]);
 else if(o==='Walker') wuaSet(pk,`Collins is doing exactly what Walker picked her to do. She's presenting herself as a moderate Republican who can work across the aisle, and when she's asked about some of Walker's more conservative positions, she openly admits that they don't agree on everything. ${n} wants to know whether to press her on those disagreements or leave Walker's record to speak for itself. What do you suggest?`,[
  {text:"Press her on it. Ask Susan why voters should believe she'll moderate a Walker administration when Scott Walker is the one who'll actually be President. If she thinks he's wrong on some of these issues, make her explain why she's helping put him in the White House.",fb:`${n} gets Collins on the defensive for a while, but she eventually gives a pretty good answer: she joined the ticket precisely because she believes presidents should surround themselves with people willing to disagree with them. Probably not the line of attack we hoped it'd be.`},
  {text:"Don't attack her for being reasonable. Tell Susan that I respect the times she's worked across the aisle, and then ask why the country should replace an administration that's already willing to do that with Scott Walker. Make this about him, not her.",fb:`Good. There's no reason to beat up Susan Collins for agreeing with us sometimes. ${n} gives her the compliment, then makes her spend the rest of the answer defending Scott Walker. That's exactly where we want her.`},
  {text:"Ask Susan what's moderate about allowing our environment to erode. We can argue about labels all night, but there's nothing moderate about weakening environmental protections and leaving the next generation to deal with the consequences. If she's supposed to be the reasonable voice on this ticket, make her explain that.",fb:`She says protecting the environment and being a "green extremist" are two different things. She argues that our administration has imposed costly rules without considering their effect on energy prices and jobs. She points to conservation and clean-energy policies she's supported herself and says there's nothing moderate about pretending Washington has the only answers. It's pretty effective. We probably should've picked a different fight.`}
 ]);
 else if(o==='Christie') wuaSet(pk,`Keane's military experience has dominated a lot of the debate, and eventually the moderator asks whether a President without military experience should defer to his generals when making decisions about war. Keane says commanders shouldn't make policy, but argues that this administration has too often ignored military advice and then blamed the results on the people wearing the uniform. ${n} is expecting him to keep pressing this point. What do you suggest?`,[
  WUA.vp==='McCaskill'
    ? {text:"Claire doesn't need any advice from me. She knows why I picked her. Let her handle Keane.",fb:`Claire knows exactly what to do. She doesn't try to out-general Keane; she goes after the billions wasted on contractors and failed projects in the wars he wanted to keep fighting. Suddenly Keane is defending not just the strategy, but what it actually cost. That's exactly why you picked her.`}
    : {text:"Don't question his service. Tell Jack I respect his experience, but generals advise and Presidents decide. I wasn't elected to automatically approve whatever the Pentagon puts on my desk. If I think sending Americans into another war is the wrong decision, it's my responsibility to say no.",fb:`Keane agrees that the President gets the final say, which sort of kneecaps his own argument. He still thinks we've made the wrong calls, obviously, but at least we're arguing about the calls now instead of whether Dennis Kucinich hates the military.`},
  {text:"Turn his experience against him. Jack Keane was one of the biggest advocates for escalating the war in Iraq when the American people wanted out. Ask him how many more times we're supposed to follow his advice into another escalation before we're allowed to question his judgment.",fb:`Keane doesn't back away from it for a second. He says the surge helped pull Iraq back from catastrophe and that refusing to change a failing strategy would've cost even more American lives. ${n} gets plenty of applause from anti-war voters, but Keane looks completely comfortable defending his record.`},
  {text:"Ask him where this ends. More troops in Afghanistan? A bigger presence in Iraq? Military action in Syria? War with Iran? If Jack thinks I've been too reluctant to use force, make him tell the country exactly where he thinks we should be using it.",fb:`This gets interesting. Keane argues that military strength can prevent wars as well as fight them. ${n} keeps pressing him on where he'd actually send American troops, and suddenly Christie spends the next day answering questions about how hawkish his ticket really is. Useful for us.`}
 ]);
 else if(o==='Trump') wuaSet(pk,`Well, we knew this was coming. Trade comes up, and Portman tries to reconcile his record as George W. Bush's Trade Representative with Trump's attacks on free-trade agreements. He argues that there's nothing inconsistent about supporting trade while demanding that China play by the rules. ${n} is getting ready to respond and wants to know how hard you want ${him} to push the contradiction. What do you suggest?`,[
  {text:"Go right at it. Donald Trump has spent this campaign telling American workers that our trade policy sold them out, and then he picked the man who served as George Bush's Trade Representative to be his Vice President. Portman was literally in charge of promoting the trade policies his running mate says helped destroy American manufacturing. Which one of them are voters supposed to believe?",fb:`That's a hell of a hit. Portman talks about China enforcement for a while, but there's really no getting around it. Trump picked a Bush trade guy while running against Bush-era trade policy. Have fun explaining that one.`},
  {text:"Don't exaggerate his record. Give Portman credit for taking China seriously when he was Trade Representative, then ask why Trump's answer is blanket tariffs instead of actually enforcing trade laws and going after specific abuses.",fb:`Portman is clearly more comfortable here. He talks about his own efforts to strengthen enforcement against China and argues that Trump is willing to go further when necessary. ${n} holds their own.`},
  {text:"Ask him plainly what he expects Trump's dangerous rhetoric will do to the economy. Trump suggested we just default on our nation's debt. That's insanity. How is anybody supposed to have confidence in the American economy when the man asking to run it talks like that?",fb:`Portman tries to minimize this, saying that's just "Donald's style," but the underlying point remains. Donald Trump is temperamentally unfit for the Presidency. We just have to hope voters consider that a deal breaker.`}
 ]);
 else if(o==='Cain') wuaSet(pk,`Abbott's leaning heavily on his record as Texas Attorney General tonight. He argues that states need somebody willing to stand up to Washington, and points to his lawsuits against our healthcare reforms, federal regulators and other administration policies as proof he's been doing exactly that. ${n} wants to know whether to go after Abbott himself or make him defend Cain. What do you suggest?`,[
  {text:"Go after the lawsuits. Abbott's spent years dragging the federal government into court every time Texas doesn't like something Washington does. Ask him whether the Vice President's job is going to be governing the country or finding new reasons to sue half of it.",fb:`${n} gets some laughs with that, but Abbott is perfectly happy to defend the lawsuits. He says his job was to defend Texas from an overreaching federal government and that he'd do every one of them again. It's a decent hit, but we're attacking something he's pretty proud of.`},
  {text:"Make it about Cain. Abbott keeps telling everyone how dangerous and unconstitutional our administration is. Fine. Ask him what Herman Cain is actually going to do differently besides repeal everything we've done. They've spent years explaining what they're against. Make them explain what they're for.",fb:`That's much more effective. Abbott starts laying out Cain's economic agenda, but ${n} keeps bringing the discussion back to what repealing our healthcare and economic policies would actually mean. Abbott wanted a debate about stopping Washington. Instead, he's stuck thinking about what he'd actually do.`},
  {text:"Ask Abbott about his supposed \"no-nonsense and zero tolerance\" approach and how that squares with Herman Cain's plan to not prosecute Wall Street and loosen financial regulations. That's not justice. It's allowing a certain level of our society amnesty because they happen to have enough money and influence.",fb:`Abbott takes issue with the language of "amnesty" being used, and he does a decent job of hitting us on using it. When pressed, however, he really doesn't have a good answer. This can only help us.`}
 ]);
 else if(o==='Brewer') wuaSet(pk,`Thune's been hammering us on the deficit tonight. He argues that Washington has proven it can't control its own spending and points to his support for a constitutional balanced-budget amendment as the only way to finally force both parties to live within their means. ${n} expects him to keep pushing it and wants to know how you want ${him} to respond. What do you suggest?`,[
  {text:"Make him explain how the numbers work. His amendment would've capped federal spending at 18 percent of the economy and made tax increases almost impossible. Ask him what gets cut to make that happen. Social Security? Medicare? Defense? Don't let him hide behind the words ‘balanced budget.’",fb:`That's effective. Thune insists Congress could eliminate waste and prioritize essential programs, but ${n} keeps asking for specifics. It's a lot easier to promise a balanced budget than explain exactly what you'd have to cut to get one.`},
  {text:"Tell him I agree that the deficit matters. We've put forward proposals to bring it down without balancing the budget on working people. Then ask why his amendment would require a two-thirds vote to raise taxes while Congress could slash programs with a simple majority. That doesn't sound much like shared sacrifice to me.",fb:`Thune argues that Washington has a spending problem, not a revenue problem, which is exactly the argument he's been making for years. ${n} gets a good exchange going over whether deficit reduction should include revenue from wealthy Americans. We score some points.`},
  {text:"John wants to lecture us about everything that's wrong with Washington? Ask him what he learned when he left Congress and became a Washington lobbyist. He was getting paid to represent special interests while he was preparing to run for the Senate. If John Thune wants to have a debate about the D.C. swamp, he should look in a mirror.",fb:`Thune doesn't run from it. He says those "special interests" were South Dakota businesses employing South Dakotans, and that he's never been ashamed of fighting for the industries his state depends on. Then he asks whether our administration thinks farmers and railroad workers are what's wrong with Washington. We had a legitimate point, but Thune makes us sound like we're attacking his state instead.`}
 ]);
 else if(o==='Palin') wuaSet(pk,`Well, this debate just took a strange turn. Bachmann was asked about abortion and immediately launched into an attack on Planned Parenthood, accusing the organization of ‘harvesting babies’ and claiming taxpayers are helping fund it. The moderator asks her what evidence she has for that claim, and Bachmann insists people need to ‘look into what Planned Parenthood is actually doing.’ ${n} looks almost as confused as everybody else and wants to know how you want ${him} to respond. What do you suggest?`,[
  {text:"Don't even dignify the harvesting babies thing. Tell Michele that if she has evidence Planned Parenthood is committing crimes, she should produce it. Otherwise we're supposed to be having a debate about who should run the country.",fb:`That's probably the right response. Bachmann insists she's raising questions the media refuses to investigate, but ${n} simply asks for evidence again. She doesn't have any. The exchange gets increasingly uncomfortable for her.`},
  {text:`Honestly? Have ${n} laugh it off. It's such an absurd thing to say that treating it like a serious policy argument just gives it credibility. Let Bachmann embarrass herself and move on.`,fb:`${n} laughs and dismisses the claim, but Bachmann immediately turns serious and says there's ‘nothing funny’ about the shape this country is in or what happens to unborn children. It's cheap, but it gets applause and gives her an easy way to recover from a bizarre statement. This ends up being a wash.`},
  WUA.vp==='McCaskill'
    ? {text:"Forget the ridiculous claim for a minute and make her defend what she actually wants to do. Planned Parenthood provides healthcare to millions of Americans, and reproductive decisions belong to women and their doctors, not Michele Bachmann or the federal government. If she wants a serious debate about abortion and women's healthcare, give her one.",fb:`She tells Bachmann that reasonable people can disagree about abortion without inventing conspiracy theories or letting politicians make private medical decisions for women. Bachmann tries to drag her back into the "harvesting" claim, but Claire refuses and keeps hammering her on what Palin and Bachmann would actually do in office. Good. Claire looks like the reasonable one here, and Bachmann looks like Bachmann.`}
    : {text:"Forget the ridiculous claim for a minute and make her defend what she actually wants to do. Planned Parenthood provides healthcare to millions of Americans, and reproductive decisions belong to women and their doctors, not Michele Bachmann or the federal government. If she wants a serious debate about abortion and women's healthcare, give her one.",fb:`Bachmann is thrilled you took the bait. She immediately turns the exchange into a passionate argument about abortion, taxpayer funding and the rights of the unborn. ${n} makes our case well enough, but this is exactly the ideological battlefield Bachmann wanted. We probably would've been better off leaving her original claim to speak for itself.`}
 ]);
 else if(o==='West') wuaSet(pk,`Brewer's leaning on her record as Governor tonight, arguing that unlike Washington politicians, she's actually had to balance a budget and make difficult decisions when the money wasn't there. She points to Arizona as proof that government can live within its means if politicians are willing to say no. ${n} expects her to keep making that argument and wants to know how you want ${him} to respond. What do you suggest?`,[
  {text:"Make her own those ‘difficult decisions.’ Arizona froze Medicaid enrollment for poor adults and tried to cut hundreds of thousands of people from coverage. If that's West and Brewer's model for balancing the federal budget, voters deserve to know what they're signing up for.",fb:`Brewer argues that Arizona was facing an enormous budget crisis and somebody had to make choices instead of spending money the state didn't have. ${n} points out that it's a lot easier to talk about spending cuts in the abstract than defend them when people start losing healthcare. That's an argument we win.`},
  {text:"Don't argue that governors never have to make cuts. Ask her what West would cut. He's the one running for President. If their campaign says Washington spends too much, make them put some numbers behind it and tell the country which programs they're actually planning to reduce.",fb:`That's smart. Brewer tries to keep the discussion on her own record, but ${n} keeps bringing it back to West's proposals. The ticket has plenty to say about runaway federal spending, but considerably less to say about exactly where all the savings are coming from. We score some points here.`},
  {text:`Have ${n} bring up the way Brewer has talked about immigration. She claimed most undocumented immigrants crossing into Arizona were drug mules, and she's talked about beheaded bodies being found in the desert. There's a difference between taking border security seriously and frightening people for political gain. Make her defend those statements.`,fb:`Brewer doesn't back down. She says it's easy for Washington politicians to criticize her when they don't have to deal with the border every day. ${n} reminds her that being Governor doesn't make something true just because she says it. We won that exchange. Arizona might disagree.`}
 ]);

 const a=wuaAnswers(pk);
 if(a.length>=3){
   const third={Daniels:-.002,Huntsman:0,Walker:-.002,Trump:.003,Cain:.003,Brewer:-.003,Palin:(WUA.vp==='McCaskill'?.005:-.0015),West:.003};
   if(Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(third,o)) wuaGlobal(a[2].pk,third[o]);
   if(o==='Huntsman'){ wuaGlobal(a[0].pk,.005); wuaGlobal(a[1].pk,-.005); wuaGlobal(a[2].pk,0); }
   if(o==='Brewer') wuaState(a[2].pk,WUA_STATE.SD,-.005);
   if(o==='West') wuaState(a[2].pk,WUA_STATE.AZ,-.006);
 }
}
function wuaBinInitial(){
 wuaSet(1193,"Dennis, we may have found Osama bin Laden. The CIA has been tracking a courier to a heavily fortified compound in Abbottabad, Pakistan. No telephone, no internet, high walls, the residents burn their own trash, and there's somebody living inside who almost never comes out. We don't have a photograph and nobody can tell us with certainty that it's him, but the intelligence community thinks there's a serious chance it is. We can send a special operations team into Pakistan without telling their government, bring the Pakistanis in on this, or keep watching the compound and try to get something more definitive. This is your call.",[
  {text:"We've been looking for this man for ten years. If we have a serious reason to believe he's in there, I'm not letting him disappear again. Don't tell Pakistan. Send the team in and bring bin Laden to justice.",fb:"You look like you're getting pale. Are you okay, sir?"},
  {text:"We're talking about sending American troops into another country's territory based on intelligence we can't even confirm. Go to the Pakistanis. Make it clear how serious we are and get their cooperation in taking him alive.",fb:"Alright. We're making the call to Islamabad. If they cooperate, we may get him alive without violating Pakistani sovereignty. If somebody tips him off, we may have just thrown away the best lead we've had in ten years. We'll know soon enough."},
  {text:"I'm not risking American lives and a crisis with Pakistan because somebody might be living in that house. Keep watching it. I want better intelligence before I authorize anything.",fb:"Fair enough, sir."}
 ]);
}
function wuaBinFollow(){
  if(WUA.binLaden==='killed'){
    wuaBinAnswerEffects({1635:.006,1636:.012,2684:0});
    wuaSet(1194,"The country has erupted. Crowds are gathering outside the White House and at Ground Zero, Republicans are congratulating the military and intelligence community, and even our opponents are admitting this is a major victory for the administration. For the first time in a long time, Americans seem genuinely united about something. You'll be addressing the country tonight. What do you want to say?",[
      {text:"This should be a somber moment, not a celebration. Osama bin Laden murdered thousands of innocent people, and justice has finally caught up with him. But his death won't bring those people back, and it shouldn't become an excuse for more war. Let's honor the victims, thank the people who carried out this mission and remind Americans that peace is still the goal.",fb:"The speech is restrained and thoughtful. Families of some victims appreciate that you refuse to turn bin Laden's death into a victory parade. A few commentators wonder why you couldn't just let the country celebrate, but the response is positive and nobody can deny who made the call."},
      {text:"Tonight is about justice and closure. Osama bin Laden spent ten years believing he could hide from the United States after murdering thousands of Americans. He was wrong. I want to thank the intelligence officers who found him, the men who carried out this operation and every American who never forgot what happened on September 11th.",fb:"This is the moment the country wanted. Even people who've questioned whether you're tough enough as Commander-in-Chief have a hard time making that case tonight. Your approval jumps, and for once Republicans don't have much of an answer. Enjoy it, Dennis. These days don't come often."}
    ]);
  } else if(WUA.binLaden==='captured'){
    wuaBinAnswerEffects({1635:.006,1636:.003,2684:0});
    wuaSet(1194,"Well, we've got Osama bin Laden. Alive. Pakistan has agreed to transfer him into American custody, and after the intelligence people finish questioning him, we have to decide what the hell we're actually going to do with him. Republicans are already demanding a military trial, the ACLU wants us to prove that even bin Laden gets due process. Whatever we choose, this trial is going to take years. There's no chance it'll be finished before the election. Where do we put him and how do we try him?",[
      {text:"He's a mass murderer, and we already have a justice system for mass murderers. What's more of a vindication of our democratic government than publicly trying a man who sought to destroy it? It'd be a beautiful thing.",fb:"Civil libertarians love it, Republicans absolutely do not. Still, with bin Laden in American custody, the argument that our justice system can handle him is a powerful one."},
      {text:"At the end of the day, he declared war on our country. Give him due process, give him a defense, but try him before a military commission.",fb:"Republicans still find things to complain about, but this is close enough to what many of them wanted that the fight never really catches fire."}
    ]);
  } else if(WUA.binLaden==='raidfail'){
    wuaBinAnswerEffects({1635:-.012,1636:-.016,2684:-.018});
    wuaSet(1194,"Dennis, this is a fucking disaster. The team made it into the compound, but bin Laden wasn't there. Nobody on our side was killed, thank God, but we've just sent American troops into Pakistan without their permission based on intelligence that turned out to be wrong. Pakistan is furious, Republicans are demanding investigations and somebody's already leaked that there were doubts inside the intelligence community about whether bin Laden was even in the compound. We need to explain this to the country. What do we say?",[
      {text:"I made the decision. The intelligence wasn't certain, I knew that when I authorized the raid, and I'm not going to blame the CIA or anybody else because it went wrong. The responsibility is mine.",fb:"Taking responsibility keeps this from becoming a blame game, but it also puts the failure squarely around your neck. Republicans don't need to invent an attack line; you just gave them one."},
      {text:"We had our best lead on Osama bin Laden in ten years. I wasn't going to sit in this office and let him disappear because taking action involved some risk. We didn't find him, but I'd make the same decision again with the information we had.",fb:"The determination helps with voters who wanted you to act, but insisting you'd do it again after the intelligence failed makes the judgment question even harder to escape."},
      {text:"I was told there was a serious chance bin Laden was inside that compound. There obviously wasn't. We need to find out how the intelligence got this wrong before anybody asks me to authorize something like this again.",fb:"The demand for answers is understandable, but intelligence officials aren't thrilled that you sound like you're pushing the failure onto them. The story turns into a nasty argument over who knew what."}
    ]);
  } else if(WUA.binLaden==='escaped'){
    wuaBinAnswerEffects({1635:-.012,1636:-.016,2684:-.010});
    wuaSet(1194,"Well, he's gone. By the time Pakistani forces reached the compound, it was empty. The CIA thinks somebody tipped them off, but we can't prove who, and Pakistan is obviously denying everything. Bin Laden and his courier have disappeared, and we have no idea where they went. Republicans are already asking why the hell we trusted Pakistan with the best lead we'd had in ten years. What do we tell the country?",[
      {text:"The decision was mine. I wasn't willing to send American troops into an allied country without their knowledge, and I still believe that was the responsible decision. It didn't work. That's on me.",fb:"You own the decision completely. That earns some respect, but it doesn't make the image of bin Laden escaping any less horrible. Republicans still pound away at us."},
      {text:"We don't know that Pakistan tipped him off, and I'm not going to accuse an ally of something this serious without evidence. We're going to keep looking for bin Laden, and we're going to find him.",fb:"It's responsible not to make accusations we can't prove, but politically this is brutal. The obvious question remains why we trusted Pakistan with the intelligence in the first place."},
      {text:"Somebody knew we were coming. I'm not going to pretend otherwise just to avoid an uncomfortable conversation with Pakistan. Suspend some of our aid and make it clear that cooperation works both ways.",fb:"The tougher response takes some heat off you and puts it onto Pakistan, although Richardson warns that blowing up the relationship could make finding bin Laden even harder."}
    ]);
  } else if(WUA.binLaden==='lost'){
    wuaBinAnswerEffects({1635:-.010,1636:0,2684:0});
    wuaSet(1194,"Dennis, we waited too long. The courier stopped appearing, activity at the compound changed almost overnight, and when the CIA finally got another look at the man they believed was bin Laden, he was gone. We have no idea where he went. The public doesn't know we ever had this lead. We can tell them now, or keep the intelligence classified while the search continues. What do you want to do?",[
      {text:"Tell them. We had a serious lead, I decided the intelligence wasn't strong enough to risk American lives, and we lost him. The American people deserve to know what happened.",fb:"This is ugly, but at least nobody can accuse us of hiding it. Republicans say your hesitation cost us bin Laden and they're going to keep saying it through Election Day."},
      {text:"Keep it classified. We're still trying to find him, and announcing exactly what we knew and when we knew it could compromise the search. This is intelligence, not campaign material.",fb:"For now, the story stays inside the government. If it stays there, we may get away with this. If it doesn't, God help us."}
    ]);
  } else if(WUA.binLaden==='wait'){
    wuaBinAnswerEffects({1635:0,1636:0,2684:0});
    wuaSet(1194,"Looks like patience paid off. After several more weeks of surveillance, the CIA finally got what we've been waiting for. They still don't have a photograph, but they've pieced together enough additional intelligence that they're now about as certain as they're ever going to be that bin Laden is inside that compound. There's always a chance they're wrong, but nobody thinks waiting longer is going to tell us much more. We're back where we started, Dennis. What do we do?",[
      {text:"We've got the confirmation I wanted. Don't tell Pakistan. Send the team in and bring bin Laden to justice.",fb:"The decision is made. The operation is moving forward."},
      {text:"Now that we're confident he's actually there, take this to Pakistan. Tell them exactly what we know and make it clear that I expect them to act immediately.",fb:"Pakistan agrees to move quickly. Now we find out whether trusting them was the right call."}
    ]);
  }
}
function wuaPostBinQuestion(){
  const b=WUA.binLaden;
  if(b==='killed' || b==='captured'){
    wuaSetSuccessTransition();
  } else if(b==='escaped'){
    wuaPostBinEffects({1643:-.004,1644:-.006});
    wuaSet(1195,"The bin Laden story isn't going away. House Republicans are demanding hearings into what Pakistan knew, when they knew it, and why we trusted them with the best lead we'd had in ten years. A few Democrats are joining calls to review military and intelligence aid to Pakistan. Richardson is warning that completely rupturing the relationship could make finding bin Laden even harder. What do you want to do?",[
      {text:"They want hearings? Fine. We'll cooperate. And while we're at it, put our aid to Pakistan under review until we know whether anyone in their government helped bin Laden escape.",fb:"The hearings are still ugly, but cooperating takes some of the cover-up language off the table. Reviewing aid also gives Democrats something tougher to say about Pakistan without making claims we can't prove."},
      {text:"We need Pakistan if we're going to find him again. Give Congress the information we can, but I'm not blowing up an alliance because Republicans want a televised trial of this administration.",fb:"Richardson agrees with the substance, but politically it sounds defensive. Republicans immediately say you're more worried about offending Pakistan than about explaining how bin Laden got away."}
    ]);
  } else if(b==='raidfail'){
    wuaPostBinEffects({1643:-.003,1644:-.006});
    wuaSet(1195,"Republicans have announced hearings into the failed Abbottabad raid. They want the intelligence assessments, the internal arguments and an explanation for why American forces were sent into Pakistan when we weren't certain bin Laden was even there. Some Democrats think an independent review may be the only way to keep this from looking like the White House investigating itself. How do you handle it?",[
      {text:"Give them the timeline and cooperate with a serious review. I made the final decision, and I don't need anyone hiding information to protect me from it.",fb:"It's still a damaging investigation, but transparency keeps it focused on the judgment call instead of turning it into a second scandal about secrecy."},
      {text:"We already review failed operations internally. I'm not handing classified intelligence to a congressional circus so they can score points on television.",fb:"There are legitimate security concerns, but this is exactly the answer Republicans hoped you'd give. Now the hearings are about the raid and what they say you're trying to hide."}
    ]);
  } else if(b==='lost'){
    wuaPostBinEffects({1643:-.004,1644:-.006});
    wuaSet(1195,"Now that the country knows we had a serious lead on bin Laden and lost it, Republicans are hammering you for waiting. They're demanding hearings into whether excessive caution cost us our best chance to find him in ten years. The intelligence community is still trying to pick up the trail, and they don't want every detail of the operation aired publicly. What do you do?",[
      {text:"Cooperate with the review and declassify what we responsibly can. I made the decision because I wasn't willing to gamble American lives on uncertain intelligence. People can judge me on that.",fb:"The hearings hurt, but your position is at least coherent. You made a cautious call, it failed, and you're willing to defend it in public."},
      {text:"Our priority is finding bin Laden, not relitigating classified intelligence on cable television. Give Congress what oversight requires and keep the rest focused on the search.",fb:"The national-security argument is legitimate, but Republicans say you're hiding behind classification after a politically disastrous decision. This remains a problem."}
    ]);
  }
}

function wuaLeakQuestion(){
  wuaPostBinEffects({1643:-.008,1644:-.012});
  wuaSet(1195,"Dennis, we've got a serious problem. Somebody inside the intelligence community leaked the Abbottabad story. The press now knows we had a probable location for Osama bin Laden, didn't act, and then lost him. Worse, they're reporting that the administration knew he'd disappeared and never told the public. We don't know who leaked it, but every network in the country is ripping us apart. Republicans are accusing us of a cover-up. How do you want to respond?",[
    {text:"We should've told the American people. I made the decision to wait because I wasn't willing to lose American lives on intelligence we couldn't confirm. When we lost him, I thought making that public would compromise the search. I still believe the first decision was right. but we should've come clean. The buck stops here.",fb:"This is still a massive disaster. We're going to be hearing about this until Election Day, but hopefully the honesty helps."},
    {text:"We don't announce every intelligence success or failure on television. We were still trying to find bin Laden, and telling him exactly what we knew would've made that harder. I understand why people are angry, but I won't apologize for not compromising our operation.",fb:"The story shifts from losing bin Laden to whether the President deliberately kept the American people in the dark. This isn't going away."}
  ]);
}
function wuaForeignDebate(pk){
  const o=WUA.opponent,b=WUA.binLaden,leak=WUA.leakResponse;
  let q='',c=[];
  if(o==='Romney'){
    q="The debate has turned to Iran and the Middle East. Romney argues that your reluctance to use American power has encouraged our enemies. “Iran is closer to a nuclear weapon, Assad is slaughtering his own people in Syria, and this President's answer has consistently been to hope somebody else solves the problem. America cannot lead from behind.” The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";
    c=[{text:"We've spent more than a decade learning where that gets us. Iran knows we will not allow them to threaten this country, but I'm going to exhaust every peaceful option before I send another American family a letter telling them their son or daughter isn't coming home.",fb:"Romney says nobody is asking for another Iraq and argues that military pressure is what makes diplomacy work. Even still, your line about another letter to a grieving family hits hard. I know there are families out there who appreciated this."},{text:"Mitt, you've called Russia our number one geopolitical enemy, you want us more involved in Syria, and you've drawn an even harder line on Iran. At some point you have to explain where all of this ends. The President's job isn't to find new enemies, it's to keep the American people safe.",fb:"Romney says recognizing threats isn't the same thing as wanting wars and accuses you of straw manning his platform. Then again, reminding people Romney is more of the same GOP foreign policy is good, even if he does defend it well."},{text:"Mitt Romney talks a lot about limited government, but apparently that ends at the Pentagon. These wars and this massive defense budget have cost us trillions while we've been told by his party that we can't afford programs to actually benefit Americans. And Governor Romney wants to spend even more. I'd ask the American people: how do you justify making government bigger and more expensive in the name of making it smaller?",fb:"Romney says a strong military isn't some wasteful government program, it's the first responsibility of the federal government. He also brings up the fact that defense contractors employ Americans that could be out of work. Republicans love that answer, but you can tell Mitt was a little uncomfortable in his response. He doesn't have a good answer on big vs small government, and that'll help us."}];
    if(b==='killed') q="The debate has turned to Iran and the Middle East. Romney gives you credit for the operation that killed Osama bin Laden, but argues that one successful operation doesn't make a foreign policy. Iran is closer to a nuclear weapon, Assad is slaughtering his own people in Syria, and he says America cannot lead from behind. The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";
    else if(b==='captured') q="The debate has turned to Iran and the Middle East. Romney acknowledges that Osama bin Laden is now in American custody, but argues that one successful operation doesn't make a foreign policy. Iran is closer to a nuclear weapon and Assad is slaughtering his own people in Syria. The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";
    else if(b==='raidfail'){q="The debate has turned to Iran and the Middle East, and Romney immediately brings up the failed Abbottabad raid. “This President sent American servicemen into a nuclear-armed country based on intelligence that turned out to be wrong. He damaged our relationship with Pakistan, Osama bin Laden is still out there, and now he tells us his judgment will keep us out of another war. Iran is closer to a nuclear weapon and Syria is burning. America needs a foreign policy more coherent than this.” The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";c[0].fb="Romney pounces on the contradiction. You say you're careful with American lives, he says, but you sent American servicemen into Pakistan on intelligence that turned out to be wrong. This is going to hurt";}
    else if(b==='escaped'){q="The debate has turned to Iran and the Middle East, and Romney immediately brings up Osama bin Laden's escape. “This President had intelligence on the location of the man responsible for September 11th. He trusted the Pakistani government with it, and he's still out there today. Now he wants us to believe that the same approach will work with Iran and Syria. Diplomacy matters, but this is just incompetence.” The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";c[0].fb="Romney has an obvious response: you exhausted the peaceful option with Pakistan and bin Laden got away. Your point about avoiding another war is still good, but this will hurt us.";}
    else if(b==='lost'){q="The debate has turned to Iran and the Middle East, and Romney immediately brings up the decision not to act on the suspected bin Laden compound. “This President had an opportunity to act against Osama bin Laden and decided to wait. Bin Laden disappeared. Now Iran is closer to a nuclear weapon and Assad is slaughtering his own people. There comes a point when a President has a duty to act.” The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";c[0].fb="Your argument about protecting American lives is powerful, but Romney has found the obvious weakness. When you had a chance to act against bin Laden, diplomacy may have allowed him to escape. That's going to hurt.";}
    else if(b==='leak') q="The debate has turned to Iran and the Middle East, and Romney immediately brings up the leaked bin Laden story. He says you had intelligence, chose not to act, bin Laden disappeared, and the administration kept it from the public. Now, he asks, why should anyone trust your judgment on Iran or Syria? The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";
  } else if(o==='Huntsman'){
    q="Huntsman has spent a lot of the debate agreeing with you that America's military operations overseas need to be reduced. Then there's a distinction. “President Kucinich and I agree that we shouldn't be nation-building in Afghanistan or involving ourselves in every conflict. Where we disagree is what comes next. I've argued for a smaller military presence focused on intelligence and counterterrorism. The President too often seems to think bringing troops home is itself a policy.” The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";
    c=[{text:"Jon, bringing our troops home isn't the end of our foreign policy. We can fight terrorism without occupying countries but strengthening our alliances and cooperation. On that, I think you and I probably agree more than you want to admit.",fb:"Huntsman argues that the differences matter when you're President. This turns into a substantive discussion, and it's hard for him to portray your foreign policy as radical, but his pitch is significantly more moderate."},{text:"You still begin with the assumption that America needs troops and bases scattered around the world to keep us safe. I don't. We have the strongest defense in the world, but that doesn't mean being the world's policeman",fb:"Huntsman says reducing America's bases is exactly what he's proposing, but that withdrawing from the world entirely isn't a foreign policy. Still, you draw a clear distinction for voters who want a much more fundamental break with the post-9/11 foreign policy model."},{text:"Jon, I hear you, but do the people you're trying to please? You've got AIPAC, CUFI and an entire Republican foreign policy establishment demanding a harder line on Iran, and you've told them yourself that all options are on the table. They hear that and believe President Huntsman will eventually give them the war they want. You don't have to be a historian to know how that ends. My question is whether you're willing to tell them no.",fb:"Huntsman laughs this off, and subtly suggests what you just rambled off was a “conspiracy theory.” Even still, the pressure from these groups for a harder line on Iran is real, and that could matter for conservatives weary of further capitulation to Israel. One problem is that's not many people. Are there any of them that'd vote for you instead anyway?"}];
    if(b==='killed') {q="Huntsman gives you credit for the operation that killed Osama bin Laden, then makes his distinction: he agrees that America should leave nation-building behind, but argues for a smaller military presence focused on intelligence and counterterrorism rather than treating withdrawal itself as a policy. The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";c[0].fb="Huntsman argues that the differences matter when you're President, but killing bin Laden is powerful evidence that counterterrorism doesn't require a massive occupation. It's hard for him to portray your approach as radical.";}
    else if(b==='captured'){q="Huntsman acknowledges that Osama bin Laden is now in American custody, then argues that bringing troops home still isn't a substitute for a smaller intelligence and counterterrorism presence overseas. The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";c[0].fb="Huntsman makes a reasonable distinction, but with bin Laden sitting in American custody it's difficult for him to argue that your approach isn't working.";}
    else if(b==='raidfail'){q="Huntsman argues that a smaller counterterrorism footprint only works with good intelligence and careful decisions, and points directly to the failed Abbottabad raid as evidence that your administration got both wrong. The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";c[0].fb="Huntsman says Abbottabad shows exactly why intelligence and cooperation matter. This is a much tougher version of his argument for us.";}
    else if(b==='escaped'){q="Huntsman brings up bin Laden's escape. “The President trusted Pakistan with intelligence about bin Laden's location, and bin Laden got away. Diplomacy and cooperation are important, but they only work when we understand who we're dealing with.” The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";c[0].fb="Pakistan is a bad example for us here, and bin Laden's escape makes our argument much harder.";c[1].fb="Huntsman says this is exactly why he supports an American presence focused on counterterrorism. He doesn't sound like a warmonger, and our failures make him sound better than he would've otherwise.";}
    else if(b==='lost'){q="Huntsman brings up your decision to wait on the suspected bin Laden compound. “I understand why the President wanted better intelligence. But we waited, bin Laden disappeared, and sometimes a President has to make a decision without knowing everything.” The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";c[0].fb="Huntsman agrees that Presidents shouldn't gamble American lives recklessly, but says waiting for a perfect opening isn't realistic either. It's a reasonable criticism and it hurts us.";}
    else if(b==='leak') q="Huntsman says he understands why intelligence sometimes has to remain classified, but asks why the American people weren't eventually told that we had a serious lead on bin Laden and lost him. The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";
  } else if(o==='Walker'){
    const armed=campaignTrail_temp.player_answers.includes(305);
    q=armed?"The debate turns to Syria, and Walker argues that your administration has allowed the situation to deteriorate while refusing to lead. “President Kucinich keeps telling us what America shouldn't do. No troops on the ground, no airstrikes, and when he finally agreed to arm the opposition, he did it without any larger strategy for defeating Assad. At some point the President has to tell us what America will do.” The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?":"The debate turns to Syria, and Walker argues that your administration has allowed the situation to deteriorate while refusing to lead. “President Kucinich keeps telling us what America shouldn't do. No troops on the ground, he doesn't want airstrikes, and he won't even arm the opposition. At some point the President has to tell us what America will do.” The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";
    c=armed?[{text:"It's easy to say the President needs to do more when you're not the one who has to decide what that is. Scott, you say what we're doing isn't enough. Alright, what would you do next? Airstrikes? American troops? How far are you prepared to take this?",fb:"Walker says he'd give the opposition everything it needs to defeat Assad. You ask him what “everything” means. He never really answers. This is not his strongest subject, and it shows."},{text:"We've isolated Assad, imposed sanctions and worked with our allies. What I won't do is pretend there's some simple American military solution to a civil war. There isn't.",fb:"Walker argues that sanctions and humanitarian aid haven't stopped Assad from killing his own people. It's a fair criticism, but he still doesn't explain what he'd do differently without getting America much more deeply involved."},{text:"Scott, you've been Governor of Wisconsin for two years. Before that, you were Milwaukee County Executive. Now you're standing here telling the President of the United States that we need to get more deeply involved in a civil war in Syria. Based on what? What experience do you have that makes you so certain you know which rebels we should arm, which ones we shouldn't, and what happens to those weapons five years from now?",fb:"Walker says you don't need a lifetime in Washington to recognize evil when you see it, and argues that your attitude is exactly what's wrong with career politicians. That's a pretty good response for him, especially with conservatives. But there's no getting around the point you raised. Walker has almost no foreign policy experience."}]:[{text:"It's easy to say the President needs to do something when you're not the one who has to decide what that is. Scott, we've armed rebels against our enemies before when our biggest enemies were the Soviets. You know how that turned out?",fb:"Walker demands you answer. You ask again if he knows. He snaps, “No I don't. Why don't you educate me and the rest of America since you're so smart?” You smile: “Al Qaeda came out of that war, Scott.” He looks immature and unprepared. Good for us!"},{text:"We've isolated Assad, imposed sanctions and worked with our allies. What I won't do is pretend there's some simple American military solution to a civil war. There isn't.",fb:"Walker argues that sanctions and humanitarian aid haven't stopped Assad from killing his own people. It's a fair criticism, but he still doesn't explain what he'd do differently without getting America much more deeply involved."},{text:"Scott, you've been Governor of Wisconsin for two years. Before that, you were Milwaukee County Executive. Now you're standing here telling the President of the United States that we need to get more deeply involved in a civil war in Syria. Based on what? What experience do you have that makes you so certain you know which rebels we should arm, which ones we shouldn't, and what happens to those weapons five years from now?",fb:"Walker says you don't need a lifetime in Washington to recognize evil when you see it, and argues that your attitude is exactly what's wrong with career politicians. That's a pretty good response for him, especially with conservatives. But there's no getting around the point you raised. Walker has almost no foreign policy experience."}];
    if(b==='killed') q="The debate turns to Syria. Walker says killing bin Laden was important, but argues that one successful raid doesn't excuse what he calls a lack of leadership while Assad kills his own people. The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";
    else if(b==='captured') q="The debate turns to Syria. Walker acknowledges that bin Laden is now in American custody, but argues that Syria still shows an administration unwilling to lead. The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";
    else if(b==='raidfail'){q="The debate turns to Syria, and Walker brings up the failed Abbottabad raid. He says you sent forces into Pakistan on intelligence that turned out to be wrong, and now refuse troops, airstrikes or broader action in Syria. The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";c[0].fb="You still expose how little Walker knows about the history, but Abbottabad weakens the knockout. He can point right back to our own failed use of force.";}
    else if(b==='escaped') q="The debate turns to Syria, and Walker says you trusted Pakistan with intelligence about bin Laden, lost him, and now expect Americans to trust your judgment about which Syrian partners deserve support. The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";
    else if(b==='lost'){q="The debate turns to Syria, and Walker brings up your decision not to act on the suspected bin Laden compound. “President Kucinich had an opportunity to act against Osama bin Laden and waited. He got away. Now Assad is slaughtering his own people while this President keeps waiting for the perfect answer. Sometimes there isn't one.” The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";c[0].fb="You expose Walker's ignorance of the history, but he has a response: nobody expected perfect certainty on bin Laden either. This should've been his weakest part of the debate, but it's a loss for us.";}
    else if(b==='leak') q="The debate turns to Syria, and Walker simply points to the leaked story: you had a possible location for bin Laden, didn't act, lost him and didn't tell the country. The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";
  } else if(o==='Christie'){
    q="The debate turns to Afghanistan, and Christie goes after your accelerated withdrawal. “The President came into office determined to get out of Afghanistan, and he was going to do it regardless of what conditions looked like on the ground. General Keane has been warning him about this for years. There's a difference between not wanting endless wars and announcing to the enemy when they can strike” The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";
    c=[{text:"Jack Keane has been telling Presidents we need more troops and more time for years. If it were up to him we'd probably still be in Vietnam. At some point somebody has to ask the obvious question: when does it end?",fb:"Christie scoffs, then says it ends when the job is finished, not when a politician decides he's tired of it. Fine. The problem is nobody seems able to tell us when the job is finished. We've been hearing some version of that answer for eleven years."},{text:"Chris, you've admitted yourself that you're not a nation-builder. You've said we've accomplished much of what we went to Afghanistan to do. So what exactly are we arguing about? Are you saying I should've left American soldiers there a little longer to appease Jack Keane?",fb:"That catches Christie off guard. He says he would've listened to commanders instead of imposing a timetable, but you've correctly pointed out that his own past statements clash with this. Keane probably isn't thrilled with this answer either."},{text:"I listened to Keane, I listened to the commanders, I listened to intelligence, and then I made the decision myself. That's what a President is elected to do. The American people didn't elect Jack Keane, and they didn't elect Chris Christie either. I'm approaching this war with the safety of our soldiers in mind, not a Fox News panel.",fb:"This is a good line, but Christie still goes on a tangent about bravery and how you can't understand it. Even still, polls after the debate showed this was a good moment for us."}];
    if(b==='killed'){q="The debate turns to Afghanistan. Christie gives you credit for getting bin Laden, but says the mission in Afghanistan was larger than one man and General Keane has spent years warning that your withdrawal was too fast. The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";c[0].fb="Christie says Presidents don't get to choose the conditions they inherit, but with bin Laden dead it's harder for him and Keane to explain exactly what objective required staying longer.";}
    else if(b==='captured'){q="The debate turns to Afghanistan. Christie acknowledges that bin Laden is now in American custody, but says General Keane has spent years warning that your accelerated withdrawal leaves unfinished business behind. The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";}
    else if(b==='raidfail'){q="The debate turns to Afghanistan, and Christie says you accelerated the withdrawal and then sent Americans into Pakistan on intelligence that turned out to be wrong. Bin Laden is still out there, and Keane has been warning about your judgment for years. The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";c[0].fb="You ask when this ends. Christie has an obvious response: when the job is finished, and bin Laden is still out there. That's a much stronger answer than usual.";}
    else if(b==='escaped'){q="The debate turns to Afghanistan, and Christie says you trusted Pakistan with the best lead on bin Laden, he escaped, and now you're accelerating the withdrawal while Keane warns that we're leaving the job unfinished. The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";}
    else if(b==='lost'){q="The debate turns to Afghanistan, and Christie says you had an opportunity to act on bin Laden, waited, lost him, and are still accelerating the withdrawal. He calls it a pattern of caution turning into hesitation. The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";}
    else if(b==='leak') q="The debate turns to Afghanistan, and Christie immediately brings up the leaked bin Laden story. “The President had a possible location for Osama bin Laden, didn't act, lost him and didn't tell the country. General Keane has spent years warning that this administration was too eager to leave Afghanistan. Turns out they lied about more than we knew.” The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";
  } else if(o==='Trump'){
    q="The debate turns to Libya, and Trump immediately goes after your handling of it. “Kucinich sat around while Gaddafi was killing people. I said we should go in, ice him, get his gold and treasure and get the hell out. We spend billions protecting everybody else and get nothing. That's why nobody respects us anymore.” The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";
    c=[{text:"Donald, you wanted us to overthrow Gaddafi and then take a bunch of gold and oil like this is an Indiana Jones sequel. Our soldiers aren't pawns for your little games.",fb:"Trump insists there's nothing wrong with getting rich for hard work. That backfires when you ask \"who's hard work?\". The audience loved the Indiana Jones line too. Good moment for us."},{text:"Half the time you say we're wasting money getting involved overseas, and the other half you're complaining that we aren't aggressive enough. Which is it? You want America out of these wars, or you want us invading Libya and for treasure?",fb:"Trump says there's no contradiction: if America gets involved, America should win and get something out of it. There's a segment of voters who like his style, but it's clear he has no clue what he's talking about."},{text:"Mr. Trump has spoken a lot about his plans, and I think that's admirable. Donald. lay it out for me in plain terms. How do we get Gaddafi, and when he's gone what do we do with Libya?",fb:"Donald actually smiles when you begin your question. He then states \"Dennis, if I told you, you'd go tell him what we'd do. You would, Dennis. So I can't say, but it's a plan many Generals and Admirals have told me is \"air-tight\" and they're all hoping we win\". How is anyone voting for this jackass?"}];
    if(b==='killed'){q="The debate turns to Libya, and Trump immediately goes after your handling of it. “Everybody talks about bin Laden. The military got bin Laden, Kucinich happened to be sitting in the White House. Congratulations. Libya was different. Gaddafi was killing people and I said go in, ice him, get his gold and treasure and get the hell out. Instead we spend billions protecting everybody else and get nothing.” The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";c[0].fb="Trump insists there's nothing wrong with getting rich for hard work. That backfires when you ask “whose hard work?” He tries to brush off the bin Laden operation too, but that only makes him look petty. The audience loved the Indiana Jones line. Good moment for us.";}
    else if(b==='captured'){q="The debate turns to Libya, and Trump immediately goes after your handling of it. “They got bin Laden and what did Kucinich do? Brought him back here, gave him lawyers and a nice little trial. I would've taken care of him right there. Libya's the same thing. Gaddafi was killing people and I said go in, ice him, get his gold and treasure and get the hell out.” The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";c[0].fb="Trump insists there's nothing wrong with getting rich for hard work. That backfires when you ask “whose hard work?” He starts talking about how bin Laden should've been killed too, but it's clear he has no clue about any of it.";}
    else if(b==='raidfail'){q="The debate turns to Libya, and Trump brings up the failed Abbottabad raid. “Abbottabad was a total disaster. Bad intelligence, bad decision, bin Laden's still out there. Now look at Libya. Kucinich sat around while Gaddafi was killing people. I said go in, ice him, get his gold and treasure and get the hell out.” The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";c[0].fb="The Indiana Jones line gets a laugh, but Trump immediately brings it back to Abbottabad. Whatever we think of his foreign policy, our failed raid makes it harder to lecture him about using American soldiers like pawns. This hurts.";}
    else if(b==='escaped'){q="The debate turns to Libya, and Trump immediately brings up Osama bin Laden. “Kucinich told Pakistan where bin Laden was and what happened? Gone. They played him for a fool. We give Pakistan billions of dollars and this is what we get. Libya's the same thing. Everybody takes advantage of us because nobody respects us anymore.” The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";}
    else if(b==='lost'){q="The debate turns to Libya, and Trump immediately brings up your decision not to act on the suspected bin Laden compound. “They had bin Laden and Kucinich waited. Who does that? You have the guy who did 9/11 sitting there and you don't go get him? Then he disappeared. It's unbelievable. Libya was the same thing: sit around, wait, talk while Gaddafi kills people.” The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";c[1].fb="Trump says he's perfectly consistent: don't fight stupid wars, but when you've got an enemy like bin Laden, finish the job. That's actually a pretty effective response, and our record handed it to him.";}
    else if(b==='leak') q="The debate turns to Libya, but Trump immediately brings up the leaked bin Laden story. “They had bin Laden, Kucinich didn't act, they lost him, and then they lied about it. It's a disgrace. And now we're supposed to trust these people on Libya, Iran, Syria? Give me a break.” The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";
  } else if(o==='Cain'){
    q="The debate turns to Iran. Cain argues that your emphasis on diplomacy has made America look weak. “Iran understands strength. They understand that this President has spent four years talking and negotiating while they get closer to a nuclear weapon. Under a Cain administration, they will know that all options are on the table.” The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";
    c=[{text:"Alright, Herman. You say all options are on the table. Which option do you want? Are you proposing we bomb Iran? Invade them? What happens the morning after? You're asking the American people to make you Commander in Chief, so tell them what you'd actually do.",fb:"Cain starts talking about consulting his military advisers and assessing the situation before making a decision. You point out that he's the one who just attacked you for doing exactly that. He never really gets out of the trap."},{text:"Herman, you've said you wouldn't make a decision until you knew all the facts and talked to the commanders on the ground. That's what Presidents do. What is it that actually makes you different here?",fb:"Cain responds, “Oh nothing much, I guess it's a little thing called common sense that you and your party are in desperate need of.” It's an effective retort, because Cain just comes off as much more down to earth to most Americans. He's still clueless, but sometimes these moments matter."},{text:"Mr. Cain certainly seems to have some interesting ideas. My question is this, what will he do with Libya?",fb:"Cain pauses for a moment, before stammering: \"What we need to do is what I said we need to do. When we took out Saddam we let his people infest the place, that was more weakness and not the strength we need\". When asked what the hell Saddam had to do with Libya, Herman says he was \"talking about the whole region\". This is good for us."}];
    if(b==='killed'){q="The debate turns to Iran. Cain gives you credit for getting Osama bin Laden, but argues that your emphasis on diplomacy has made America look weak elsewhere. “Getting bin Laden was important, but Iran understands strength. They've spent four years getting closer to a nuclear weapon while this President talks. Under a Cain administration, they'll know that all options are on the table.” The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";c[0].fb="Cain starts talking about consulting his military advisers and assessing the situation before making a decision. You point out that he's the one who just attacked you for doing exactly that. Getting bin Laden makes his argument even harder to explain.";}
    else if(b==='captured'){q="The debate turns to Iran. Cain acknowledges bringing Osama bin Laden into custody, but argues that your emphasis on diplomacy has made America look weak elsewhere. “Bin Laden is going to face justice, and that's good. But Iran understands strength. Under a Cain administration, they'll know that all options are on the table.” The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";c[0].fb="Cain falls back on consulting his military advisers before deciding what he'd actually do. With bin Laden sitting in American custody, it's pretty difficult for him to argue that our approach isn't working.";}
    else if(b==='raidfail'){q="The debate turns to Iran, and Cain brings up the failed Abbottabad raid. “We sent Americans into Pakistan on bad intelligence, the operation failed and bin Laden is still out there. That's what happens when you don't have clarity. Iran needs to know exactly where America stands.” The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";c[0].fb="You get Cain talking about consulting commanders and intelligence before making decisions, but this time he has an obvious response: maybe we should've done more of that before Abbottabad.";c[1].fb="Cain responds, “Oh nothing much, I guess it's a little thing called common sense that you and your party are in desperate need of.” He then points to Abbottabad as an example of what he means. He's light on details, but we still look worse.";}
    else if(b==='escaped'){q="The debate turns to Iran, and Cain brings up Osama bin Laden's escape. “We trusted Pakistan with intelligence about bin Laden and he disappeared. That's exactly the problem with this President's foreign policy. We don't even know who our friends are. Now he wants to use the same kind of diplomacy with Iran.” The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";c[1].fb="Cain responds, “Oh nothing much, I guess it's a little thing called common sense that you and your party are in desperate need of.” He points straight to Pakistan. It's vague, but after what happened with bin Laden the audience knows exactly what he's talking about.";}
    else if(b==='lost'){q="The debate turns to Iran, and Cain brings up your decision not to act on the suspected bin Laden compound. “We had a chance to get Osama bin Laden and this President waited. Now he's gone. That's what happens when you spend so much time thinking about what could go wrong that you forget to act.” The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";c[1].fb="Cain responds, “Oh nothing much, I guess it's a little thing called common sense that you and your party are in desperate need of.” He says common sense would've told him not to let bin Laden get away. It's not much of a policy answer, but the people are on his side on this one.";}
    else if(b==='leak') q="The debate turns to Iran, but Cain brings up the leaked bin Laden story. “We had a chance to get Osama bin Laden, the President didn't act, bin Laden disappeared, and then they didn't tell us. Before we talk about clarity with Iran, how about some clarity with the American people?” The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";
  } else if(o==='Brewer'){
    q="The debate turns to Pakistan. Brewer argues that your administration has been far too willing to send money to governments that don't respect the United States. “We give Pakistan billions of dollars while terrorists operate right under their noses. In Arizona, I learned that when people refuse to enforce the law, you don't reward them for it. Washington could use some of that common sense.” The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";
    c=[{text:"Jan, Pakistan has nuclear weapons. This isn't Arizona. What exactly are you proposing? Cut them off tomorrow? Threaten them? Send troops in? You're running for President now. Tell us what you'd actually do.",fb:"Brewer says she'd condition every dollar on Pakistani cooperation. You ask what happens when they say no. She says America has to get tough. You ask what “get tough” means. She never really answers. Conditioning aid is popular, though, and we need to remember that."},{text:"Comparing Pakistan to Arizona is ridiculous. Foreign policy is complicated, Jan. You can't build a fence around every country we have a problem with, and international relations aren't fixed by scaring everyone.",fb:"Brewer shoots back, “Maybe if Washington had built one around Arizona, we wouldn't have the problem we do.” The audience laughs, and suddenly we're debating the border. This was bad."},{text:"You're right that we've poured money into Pakistan, but I'm the one who's been trying to change the system where Washington props up governments and militaries overseas. A thank you note would've been nice, Jan, since you seem to care a lot about this. Where have you been?",fb:"Brewer responds by telling you she's been protecting Arizona. Still, she really can't get you on this. At the end of the day, it's clear shes light on specifics because she doesn't have any."}];
    if(b==='killed'){q="The debate turns to Pakistan. Brewer acknowledges that you got Osama bin Laden, but argues that finding him there proves her point. “We found the world's most wanted terrorist living right under Pakistan's nose while we're giving them billions of dollars. President Kucinich got bin Laden, and I'll give him credit for that. But why are we still rewarding a government that either couldn't find him or didn't want to?” The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";c[0].fb="Brewer says she'd condition every dollar on Pakistani cooperation. You ask what happens when they say no; she says America has to get tough, but never defines it. Conditioning aid still plays well, but with bin Laden dead it's harder for her to argue our approach hasn't produced results.";c[1].fb="Brewer shoots back, “Maybe if Washington had built one around Arizona, we wouldn't have the problem we do.” The audience laughs, and suddenly we're debating the border. Still, with bin Laden dead, her argument that our foreign policy simply isn't working doesn't carry as much weight.";}
    else if(b==='captured'){q="The debate turns to Pakistan. Brewer acknowledges that Osama bin Laden is now in American custody, but argues that finding him there proves her point. “We found the world's most wanted terrorist living right under Pakistan's nose while we're giving them billions of dollars. I'm glad we got him, but somebody needs to explain why American taxpayers are still rewarding a government that either couldn't find him or didn't want to.” The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";c[0].fb="Brewer says she'd put conditions on every dollar and demand that Pakistan cooperate with us. You keep pressing her on what happens when they refuse, and she never really answers that. With bin Laden sitting in American custody, we've got a pretty powerful example that our approach can work.";c[1].fb="Brewer shoots back, “Maybe if Washington had built one around Arizona, we wouldn't have the problem we do.” The audience laughs, but there's an obvious problem with her larger argument: bin Laden is sitting in American custody. We stumbled into her favorite subject, but this isn't nearly as damaging as it normally would be.";}
    else if(b==='raidfail'){q="The debate turns to Pakistan, and Brewer immediately brings up the failed Abbottabad raid. “President Kucinich sent Americans into a nuclear-armed country on intelligence that turned out to be wrong. The operation failed, bin Laden is still out there, and now our relationship with Pakistan is worse than ever. That's what happens when Washington has no idea how to deal with people who don't respect us.” The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";c[0].fb="Brewer says maybe asking those questions before sending Americans into Pakistan would've been a good idea. She still doesn't explain what she'd actually do differently going forward, but Abbottabad makes our attempt to expose that weakness pretty ineffective.";c[1].fb="Brewer shoots back, “Maybe if Washington had built one around Arizona, we wouldn't have the problem we do.” The audience laughs, and then she brings it right back to Abbottabad. Now we're losing an argument about Pakistan and debating the border at the same time. This was really bad.";}
    else if(b==='escaped'){q="The debate turns to Pakistan, and Brewer immediately brings up Osama bin Laden's escape. “This President had intelligence on bin Laden and what did he do? He gave it to Pakistan. Then bin Laden disappeared. We give them billions of dollars and they either can't help us or won't help us. In Arizona, I learned that when people refuse to enforce the law, you don't reward them for it.” The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";c[1].fb="Brewer shoots back, “Maybe if Washington had built one around Arizona, we wouldn't have the problem we do.” The audience laughs. Then she reminds everyone that you trusted Pakistan and bin Laden disappeared. This was a disaster.";}
    else if(b==='lost'){q="The debate turns to Pakistan, and Brewer brings up your decision not to act on the suspected bin Laden compound. “We thought we knew where Osama bin Laden was, President Kucinich waited, and now he's gone. Meanwhile we're still sending billions of dollars to Pakistan while terrorists operate inside their borders. At some point America has to stop being afraid to demand something in return.” The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";c[1].fb="Brewer shoots back, “Maybe if Washington had built one around Arizona, we wouldn't have the problem we do.” The audience laughs. Worse, she says building a fence isn't the point; knowing when somebody isn't cooperating with you is. With bin Laden gone, this was bad.";}
    else if(b==='leak') q="The debate turns to Pakistan, and Brewer immediately brings up the leaked bin Laden story. “This President thought he knew where Osama bin Laden was, didn't act, lost him, and then didn't tell the American people. Meanwhile we're sending billions of dollars to Pakistan. Before President Kucinich lectures anybody about accountability, maybe he should explain where his own is.” The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";
  } else if(o==='West'){
    q="The debate turns to Afghanistan. West argues that your withdrawal has ignored the larger threat facing the country. “President Kucinich thinks bringing the troops home means the war is over. It doesn't. Radical Islam declared war on the United States long before Afghanistan, and we will defeat them instead of cutting and running.” The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";
    c=[{text:"So tell the American people where this war ends. Afghanistan? Pakistan? Iran? Syria? How many countries are you prepared to send Americans into before you finally feel safe?",fb:"West says you're deliberately confusing fighting terrorism with invading countries and that he'll use every tool available to defend America. You ask him again where the war ends. He never gives you an answer. That was exactly the point."},{text:"Allen, I've been Commander in Chief for a while now. I've had to make these decisions with American lives on the line. Giving speeches about strength is easy. Deciding when somebody's son or daughter goes to war is a lot harder, and I don't treat it like it's nothing.",fb:"West reminds you that he actually led American soldiers in combat while you were voting in Congress. Whatever you think of his foreign policy, questioning whether he understands the consequences of war was a terrible idea."},{text:"Congressman West talks a lot about his military service, so let's talk about it. He was relieved of his command after threatening an Iraqi detainee and firing his pistol next to the man's head during an interrogation. That's not a question of whether you're tough enough. That's a question of judgment. And I think the American people should seriously consider whether they want a man who made a decision like that with access to the nuclear codes.",fb:"West is furious. He says he believed his soldiers' lives were in danger and that he'd make the same decision again if it meant protecting the men under his command. He then accuses you of using an incident from his military service for political gain. You question whether a man who loses his temper that easily just proved your point. By the time the moderator finally cuts both of you off, neither one of you looks particularly presidential."}];
    if(b==='killed'){q="The debate turns to Afghanistan. West gives you credit for getting Osama bin Laden, but argues that your withdrawal has ignored the larger threat facing the country. “Killing bin Laden was an important victory, but President Kucinich seems to think that means the war is over. It doesn't. Radical Islam declared war on the United States long before Afghanistan, and we will defeat them instead of cutting and running.” The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";c[0].fb="West says killing one terrorist doesn't eliminate the larger threat and accuses you of deliberately confusing fighting terrorism with invading countries. You ask him again where his war ends. He never gives you an answer, and with bin Laden dead it's harder for him to make his case.";c[1].fb="West reminds you that he actually led American soldiers in combat while you were voting in Congress. That gives him a powerful retort, but since we got bin Laden, the attack doesn't carry nearly as much weight.";}
    else if(b==='captured'){q="The debate turns to Afghanistan. West acknowledges that Osama bin Laden is now in American custody, but argues that your withdrawal has ignored the larger threat facing the country. “Capturing bin Laden was important, but this war was never about one man. Radical Islam declared war on the United States long before Afghanistan, and we will defeat them instead of cutting and running.” The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";c[1].fb="West reminds you that he actually led American soldiers in combat while you were voting in Congress. It's still a good comeback, but with bin Laden in American custody his case is harder to make.";}
    else if(b==='raidfail'){q="The debate turns to Afghanistan, and West immediately brings up the failed Abbottabad raid. “President Kucinich tells us how seriously he takes sending Americans into harm's way, but he sent our forces into Pakistan on intelligence that turned out to be wrong. The mission failed and bin Laden is still out there. Now he wants us to believe that retreating from Afghanistan will make us safer.” The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";c[0].fb="West says nobody is proposing invading every country you listed and argues that the problem is knowing when and where to use force. You still expose how vague he is, but with our foiled bin Laden operation we still look bad here.";c[1].fb="West reminds you that he actually led American soldiers in combat, then points out that your own decision sent Americans into Pakistan on bad intelligence. We walked straight into this one.";}
    else if(b==='escaped') q="The debate turns to Afghanistan, and West immediately brings up Osama bin Laden's escape. “President Kucinich had intelligence on bin Laden's location and trusted Pakistan with it. What happened? Bin Laden disappeared. This is a government we've given billions of dollars to while our enemies operate inside its borders. That's not diplomacy. That's weakness.” The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";
    else if(b==='lost'){q="The debate turns to Afghanistan, and West immediately brings up your decision not to act on the suspected bin Laden compound. “President Kucinich had an opportunity to get Osama bin Laden and he waited. Now bin Laden is gone. That's the problem with this administration. Our enemies don't stop and wait while the President worries about making a difficult decision.” The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";c[0].fb="West says nobody is asking you to invade every country you listed, but when you locate the man responsible for September 11th, you act. You still force him to defend his much broader view of the war, but the people are with him on bin Laden.";}
    else if(b==='leak'){q="The debate turns to Afghanistan, and West immediately brings up the leaked bin Laden story. “President Kucinich had a possible location for Osama bin Laden. He didn't act, bin Laden disappeared, and then his administration didn't tell the American people. Now he wants to lecture us about judgment and responsibility in war.” The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";if(leak==='defend')c[0].fb="West says this isn't about how many countries he'd invade, it's about whether the Commander in Chief can be trusted. He keeps coming back to the fact that bin Laden escaped and you kept it from the public.";}
  } else if(o==='Palin'){
    q="The debate turns to Iran. Palin argues that you've spent four years showing America's enemies that we are weak. “Iran is working toward a nuclear weapon, they've threatened Israel with a new Holocaust. The President's answer is always trying to negotiate with terrorists. Sometimes you have to show the bad guys that America isn't afraid to fight.” The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";
    c=[{text:"We're told there's an enemy who can't be negotiated with, there's an imminent threat, and military action is the only thing they'll understand. We just went through this with Iraq. And if you want to talk about negotiation, the Israelis aren't negotiating at all, they're threatening to attack.",fb:"Okay, so this is accurate. However, comparing the Israelis to terrorists that cannot be negotiated with is probably the wrong move. This just gives Sarah all the ammunition she needed."},{text:"Alright Sarah, let's say you're President tomorrow. Iran keeps enriching uranium. What do you do? Bomb them? Invade them? How exactly does President Palin stop Iran from getting a nuclear weapon?",fb:"Palin says she'd tighten sanctions, support Israel and keep military action on the table. You press for specifics; she asks whether you're so desperate to know because you don't have a plan. The audience eats it up. She stumbles later, keeping this from getting worse, but this wasn't good."},{text:"Israel's aggression toward its neighbors doesn't make Israelis safer. It makes the region more dangerous for everybody. The United States should be playing the role of a mediator in the Middle East, not a bully. And if Israel is our friend, then we ought to be honest with them when we think they're making a mistake. That's what friends do.",fb:"Palin says you're taking America on another “apology tour” and that Israel needs a friend in the White House, not another enemy. The apology line falls pretty flat. Most Americans support Israel, but your basic argument is sensible enough to resonate with some voters who want the United States to prevent further conflict."}];
    if(b==='killed'){q="The debate turns to Iran. Palin acknowledges that you got Osama bin Laden, but argues that one successful operation doesn't change the larger problem. “Iran is working toward a nuclear weapon, they've threatened Israel with a new Holocaust. Killing bin Laden was a victory, but the President's answer to every other threat is trying to negotiate with terrorists. Sometimes you have to show the bad guys that America isn't afraid to fight.” The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";c[0].fb="Palin jumps all over the comparison between Israel and terrorists, and that was still a terrible thing to hand her. But with bin Laden dead, it's much harder for her to argue that your approach to national security is simply weakness. This could've been worse.";c[1].fb="Palin says she'd tighten sanctions, support Israel and keep military action on the table. You press for specifics; she asks whether you're desperate to know because you don't have a plan. The audience likes the comeback, but with bin Laden dead it's harder for her to argue you won't act when necessary.";}
    else if(b==='captured'){q="The debate turns to Iran. Palin acknowledges that Osama bin Laden is now in American custody, but argues that the larger threats haven't disappeared. “I'm glad we got bin Laden, but Iran is working toward a nuclear weapon and they've threatened Israel with a new Holocaust. The President's answer is always trying to negotiate with terrorists. Sometimes you have to show the bad guys that America isn't afraid to fight.” The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";c[0].fb="Palin immediately attacks the comparison between Israel and terrorists. That's still bad. But with bin Laden sitting in American custody, her argument that your approach to America's enemies isn't working is harder to make.";c[1].fb="Palin says she'd tighten sanctions, support Israel and keep military action on the table. When you demand specifics, she asks why you're so desperate to know, and asks if it's because you don't have a plan. It gets a reaction, but with bin Laden in American custody her case is harder to make.";}
    else if(b==='raidfail'){q="The debate turns to Iran, and Palin brings up the failed Abbottabad raid. “The President sent our finest into Pakistan on bad intelligence, the mission failed and bin Laden is still out there. Now Iran is working toward a nuclear weapon and we're supposed to trust his judgment again? Sometimes you have to know when to fight and how to win.” The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";c[0].fb="Palin immediately attacks the comparison between Israel and terrorists, then brings up Abbottabad as evidence that your judgment is hardly beyond question. We gave her two attacks for the price of one. This was awful.";c[1].fb="Palin says she'd tighten sanctions, support Israel and keep military action on the table. When you push her for specifics, she asks whether Abbottabad was your idea of having a plan. She still hasn't really answered the question, but nobody cares. We look worse.";}
    else if(b==='escaped'){q="The debate turns to Iran, and Palin immediately connects it to Osama bin Laden's escape. “The President trusted Pakistan with intelligence about bin Laden and what happened? He disappeared. Now he wants us to trust negotiation with Iran while they're working toward a nuclear weapon and threatening Israel with a new Holocaust. How many times do we have to get burned before we learn?” The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";}
    else if(b==='lost'){q="The debate turns to Iran, and Palin immediately brings up your decision not to act on the suspected bin Laden compound. “President Kucinich thought he knew where Osama bin Laden was and waited. Now he's gone. Iran is working toward a nuclear weapon, they've threatened Israel with a new Holocaust, and this President still thinks every problem can be solved by waiting and talking.” The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";c[1].fb="Palin says she'd tighten sanctions, support Israel and keep military action on the table. You demand to know exactly when she'd use force, and she asks whether you would've liked a few more years to think about bin Laden too. The audience eats it up. This was bad.";}
    else if(b==='leak') q="The debate turns to Iran, but Palin immediately brings up the leaked bin Laden story. “President Kucinich thought he knew where Osama bin Laden was, didn't act, lost him and then didn't tell the American people. Now Iran is working toward a nuclear weapon and he wants us to trust his judgment again. Why should we?” The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";
  } else {
    q="The debate has turned to Afghanistan and America's role in the world. Daniels acknowledges that the country is tired of war but argues your foreign policy has gone too far in the other direction. “There is a difference between avoiding wars and convincing our allies that America may simply walk away when things get tough. From Afghanistan to Libya to Syria, this President has had the United States playing second fiddle.” The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";
    c=[{text:"I promised the American people I would end these wars, and I've kept that promise. I've used force when it was necessary, diplomacy when it could work, and I've stopped treating American soldiers like pawns on a game board. That's not weakness. That's what a President is supposed to do.",fb:"Daniels argues that the problem isn't ending wars, it's what happens after we leave. Still, it's difficult for him to paint you as weak when he can't explain how he'd end the war differently."},{text:"Mitch Daniels worked for George W. Bush while this country was being marched into Iraq. We were told that war would be quick and painless. It wasn't. I'm not going to apologize for learning from that disaster and being a hell of a lot more careful with American lives.",fb:"Daniels was ready for the Bush attack. He reminds everyone that he ran the budget office, not the Pentagon. He also says four years as President should be enough time to defend your own decisions without blaming your predecessor. This was a bad moment for us."},{text:"America is strongest when the rest of the world respects us. We can defend this country while also seeking diplomatic solutions where we can. That's the foreign policy I've tried to build.",fb:"Daniels points out that he's questioned the cost of America's foreign commitments himself, and the two of you actually sound closer on this question than either campaign probably wanted. This may make some neo-cons nervous about him, which could help us this late in the race."}];
    if(b==='killed') q="The debate has turned to Afghanistan and America's role in the world. Daniels gives you credit for the operation that killed Osama bin Laden, but argues that one success doesn't erase a foreign policy that has too often left allies wondering whether America will walk away when things get tough. The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";
    else if(b==='captured') q="The debate has turned to Afghanistan and America's role in the world. Daniels acknowledges that Osama bin Laden is now in American custody, but argues that one success doesn't erase a foreign policy that has too often left allies wondering whether America will walk away when things get tough. The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";
    else if(b==='raidfail') q="The debate has turned to Afghanistan and America's role in the world. Daniels points to the failed Abbottabad raid and argues that restraint means little if the operations you do authorize are based on bad intelligence. The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";
    else if(b==='escaped') q="The debate has turned to Afghanistan and America's role in the world. Daniels points to bin Laden's escape after you warned Pakistan and argues that diplomacy only works when America's partners can actually be trusted. The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";
    else if(b==='lost') q="The debate has turned to Afghanistan and America's role in the world. Daniels points to your decision to wait on the suspected bin Laden compound, after which bin Laden disappeared, and argues that restraint can become hesitation. The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";
    else if(b==='leak') q="The debate has turned to Afghanistan and America's role in the world. Daniels brings up the leaked bin Laden story and says the issue is no longer only judgment, but whether the public can trust what your administration tells them. The moderator turns to you. How do you respond?";
  }
  q="It\'s the night of the final presidential debate. "+q;
  wuaSet(pk,q,c);
  const bases={Daniels:[0.004,-0.006,0.003],Romney:[-0.002,0.002,0.004],Huntsman:[0.002,-0.004,0.002],Walker:[0.006,0.001,0.003],Christie:[0.003,0.005,0.003],Trump:[0.006,0.002,0.004],Cain:[0.006,0,0.004],Brewer:[-0.003,-0.006,0.003],West:[0.005,-0.006,0],Palin:[-0.007,-0.004,0.002]};
  let d=(b==='killed'||b==='captured')?0.004:b==='raidfail'?-0.004:b==='escaped'?-0.004:b==='lost'?-0.005:b==='leak'?(leak==='admit'?-0.006:-0.008):0;
  const a=wuaAnswers(pk),base=bases[o]||bases.Daniels; a.forEach((x,i)=>wuaGlobal(x.pk,(base[i]||0)+d));
  if(o==='Huntsman'&&a[2]){wuaState(a[2].pk,WUA_STATE.IN,-.004);['SC','GA','AL','MS','LA','AR','TN','KY','NC','VA','FL','TX'].forEach(x=>wuaState(a[2].pk,WUA_STATE[x],-.002));}
}

function wuaImpeachmentEligible(){
  // "Unilateral" route is the exact three-choice chain already present:
  // second recovery through reconciliation (2), TARP auto rescue (86),
  // and the Fourteenth Amendment debt-ceiling route (175).
  const A=campaignTrail_temp.player_answers || [];
  return (
    WUA.midterm==='wave' &&
    A.includes(2) &&
    A.includes(86) &&
    A.includes(175) &&
    ApprovalRating < 40 &&
    NationalUnity <= -10
  );
}

function wuaApplyImpeachmentQ1(){
  const q=wuaQ(37);
  const slots=campaignTrail_temp.answers_json.filter(x=>Number(x.__wuaHome)===37).slice(0,3);
  if(!q || slots.length<3) return false;

  q.fields.description=`Republicans have control of both chambers now, and they're wasting no time.

The House Judiciary Committee has opened an impeachment inquiry into your administration's use of executive authority. Republicans are pointing to the recovery package, the auto bailout and, most recently, your decision to act unilaterally during the debt ceiling crisis. Individually, we've defended every one of those decisions as lawful. Republicans say that taken together they show something else: a President who decided that whenever Congress told him no, he could simply find another way to do it himself.

Under better circumstances, maybe this goes nowhere. These aren't better circumstances.

Your approval rating has collapsed. The country is exhausted after three years of almost nonstop political warfare, and even Democratic voters are beginning to show signs of fatigue. A handful of congressional Democrats have already said publicly that the investigation is legitimate. More are saying the same thing privately.

They're going to impeach you, Dennis. The question is what we're going to look like when they do.`;

  const choices=[
    {
      text:`Congress doesn't get to manufacture a constitutional crisis because they lost policy fights with the President. Every action we took was to keep people working, keep the economy alive and keep the United States from defaulting on its obligations. I'm not apologizing for any of it, and I'm sure as hell fighting.`,
      fb:`I didn't expect anything less. But neither did the GOP. The House votes to impeach you largely along party lines. A few Democrats join them.`
    },
    {
      text:`I'm willing to acknowledge that some of these decisions pushed executive authority further than I ever wanted. But we were dealing with emergencies, not some law school hypothetical. We'll cooperate completely, and we'll make our case to the American people.`,
      fb:`You give them everything they ask for. Documents, lawyers, testimony, the works. It doesn't matter. Republicans call your cooperation proof that their investigation was justified and impeach you anyway. Several Democrats join them.`
    },
    {
      text:`Take this directly to the people. I want an address from the Oval Office. They want to put me on trial for saving the auto industry, preventing a depression and refusing to let the United States default? Fine. Let them explain that to the people who would've paid the price if we'd done nothing. THAT is what they need to hear, not some nonsense cooked up by reactionaries.`,
      fb:`Polling right after your address did show your favorables shoot up a little, but it doesn't change anything really. The House impeaches you, with some Democratic votes too. This isn't good.`
    }
  ];

  slots.forEach((a,i)=>{
    a.fields.question=37;
    a.fields.description=choices[i].text;
    wuaFeedback(a.pk,choices[i].fb);
  });
  campaignTrail_temp.answers_json
    .filter(x=>Number(x.fields.question)===37 && !slots.includes(x))
    .forEach(x=>x.fields.question=-99001);

  WUA.impeachmentQ1Answers=slots.map(x=>Number(x.pk));
  return true;
}

function wuaApplyImpeachmentQ2(){
  const q=wuaQ(1201);
  const slots=campaignTrail_temp.answers_json.filter(x=>Number(x.__wuaHome)===1201);
  const a=slots[0];
  if(!q || !a) return false;

  q.fields.description=`Democratic leaders come through the Oval Office, always careful with their words. Nobody tells you outright that you're going to be convicted. They don't have to. Senators who were defending you a month ago are talking about “putting the country first” and “beginning the healing process.” Others won't return calls from the White House at all.

The Constitution requires two-thirds of the Senate to remove a President. A few months ago, Republicans weren't close. Tonight, nobody in this building is willing to promise you that they still don't have the votes.

And underneath everything else is 2012. Nobody says that part either.

Russ is the last person to come see you.

He closes the door behind him and sits across from your desk. For a while neither of you says anything.

“I know what they're asking you to do,” he says. “And I think you know what needs to be done.”

You look around the Oval Office. Four years ago, getting here seemed impossible. For three years you've told yourself that every fight was worth having if somebody was better off because you had it.

There aren't many fights left now.`;

  a.fields.question=1201;
  a.fields.description=`My fellow Americans, when I took the oath of office nearly three years ago, I promised that this government would not stand idle while working people lost their jobs, their homes and their futures. Every decision I made as President, whether popular or unpopular, was made with that promise in mind. I believed then, and I believe tonight, that when this country faced extraordinary circumstances, doing nothing simply because doing something was politically difficult would have been a far greater failure.

I know that some believe I exceeded the authority entrusted to this office. I do not agree. I will not pretend tonight that I regret fighting for the people who sent me here, or that I believe the intentions behind those decisions were wrong.

But the presidency does not belong to Dennis Kucinich. It belongs to the American people. And I cannot ask this country to endure another year consumed by a fight over whether I should remain in this office.

Therefore, effective at noon tomorrow, I will resign the office of President of the United States.`;

  wuaFeedback(a.pk,`For a few seconds after the cameras shut off, nobody says anything.

Then Russ stands up.

Tomorrow afternoon, he'll be President.`);

  slots.slice(1).forEach(x=>x.fields.question=-99001);
  campaignTrail_temp.answers_json
    .filter(x=>Number(x.fields.question)===1201 && x!==a)
    .forEach(x=>x.fields.question=-99001);

  WUA.impeachmentQ2Answer=Number(a.pk);
  return true;
}

function wuaApplyFeingoldElection(){
  WUA.impeachmentResigned=true;

  // Feingold succeeds Kucinich; Trump/Portman is the authored Republican ticket.
  setCandidateIdentity(16,{first:"Russ",last:"Feingold"});
  WUA.opponent='Trump';
  wuaSyncOpponentIdentity();

  // CTS caches the player's display name separately in some builds.
  campaignTrail_temp.candidate_first_name="Russ";
  campaignTrail_temp.candidate_last_name="Feingold";
  campaignTrail_temp.candidate_name="Russ Feingold";

  // Start from the world the player created, then make the post-resignation
  // election substantially more Republican. Double Johnson/Stein support.
  (campaignTrail_temp.candidate_state_multiplier_json || []).forEach(row=>{
    const c=Number(row?.fields?.candidate);
    const v=Number(row?.fields?.state_multiplier);
    if(!Number.isFinite(v)) return;
    // Keep the player's existing map recognizable. The first test's 0.78/1.22
    // swing was far too large and produced an almost 50-state Trump landslide.
    if(c===16) row.fields.state_multiplier=v*0.995;
    if(c===17) row.fields.state_multiplier=v*1.005;
    if(c===18 || c===19) row.fields.state_multiplier=v*2;
  });

  // The next normal advance should go straight to election night rather than
  // resuming Kucinich's 2011-12 question sequence.
  try{
    const last=campaignTrail_temp.questions_json.length-1;
    if(typeof campaignTrail_temp.question_number==="number") campaignTrail_temp.question_number=last;
    if(typeof question_number==="number") question_number=last;
  }catch(e){}
}
// Q39/Q40 assassination route: use two adjacent, already-existing question slots.
// Prepare both when Q1194 is answered; never rewrite Q1202 (jobs report).
function wuaApplyAssassinationQ39Q40(){
  const q1=wuaQ(1195), q2=wuaQ(1196);
  const first=campaignTrail_temp.answers_json.find(x=>Number(x.pk)===1643);
  const second=campaignTrail_temp.answers_json.find(x=>Number(x.pk)===1649);
  const third=campaignTrail_temp.answers_json.find(x=>Number(x.pk)===1650);
  if(!q1||!q2||!first||!second||!third) return false;
  q1.fields.description=`You're lying in a hospital bed in Manchester, New Hampshire. A few hours ago, you were speaking at a campaign rally when a man in the crowd pulled out a gun and opened fire. Secret Service agents were on him almost immediately, but not before several shots were fired.

You were extraordinarily lucky. One bullet grazed your arm without striking the bone, and the doctors expect you to make a full recovery. Elizabeth was with you at the rally, but she was nowhere near the gunfire and wasn't hurt. The shooter was killed before he could do any more damage.

One of the Secret Service agents who threw himself between you and the gunman wasn't as lucky. He died from his wounds shortly after arriving at the hospital.

The country is in shock. Both campaigns have suspended their events, crowds have gathered outside the hospital, and every network in America has been covering virtually nothing else since the shooting. Russ is already back in Washington. In situations like this, nobody takes chances with the President and Vice President being in the same place.

Elizabeth and Joe Trippi are sitting beside your bed. For the first time since the shooting, the room has gone quiet.

“What happens now?”`;
  first.fields.question=1195;
  first.fields.description="What happens now?";
  campaignTrail_temp.answers_json.filter(x=>Number(x.fields.question)===1195 && Number(x.pk)!==1643)
    .forEach(x=>x.fields.question=-99001);
  wuaFeedback(1643,"Right now? You stay here. The campaign can wait.");
  wuaSetGlobalEffect(1643,.060);
  wuaNeutralizeIssueEffects(1643);

  q2.fields.description=`It's been a week since the shooting in New Hampshire. Aside from the cast on your arm, you're doing remarkably well. The doctors aren't thrilled about it, but they've cleared you to leave the hospital, and the campaign is preparing for your return.

The country is still trying to process what happened. The Secret Service agent killed protecting you was laid to rest two days ago. Vigils have been held across the country, and for a few days at least, some of the usual political noise seems to have stopped. Your poll numbers have jumped six points since the shooting.

Investigators have spent the week going through the gunman's life, computer and writings. What they've found is an angry man obsessed with politics who had spent months writing about you online before deciding to kill you. There's no evidence he was acting with anyone else.

Trump condemned the shooting immediately and called Elizabeth from the campaign trail that night. Since then, though, he's been getting increasingly irritated by questions about whether his rhetoric contributed to the atmosphere surrounding the attack. Reporters have dug up speeches where he's called you a traitor, accused you of deliberately destroying the country and told crowds that Americans needed to “take their country back” before it was too late.

Trump says blaming him for the actions of a murderer is disgusting. He also let this doozy slip during a press event when the subject came up:

“No, my campaign is about love, you understand that? Love for America, love for economy and love for military. And, it's not me by the way, but I heard someone say it, they said that the timing sure is strange, right? Really strange, because we were winning and winning and winning and all of a sudden, a thing like that happens. But we are about love, darling. Love.”

And unlike when he usually says something like this, the “someone” he heard “say something” was a real person: radio personality Alex Jones. I didn't even want to bother you with this crap, but some people are afraid this could encourage more violence.

You're leaving the hospital this afternoon. What do you want to say?`;
  second.fields.question=1196;
  second.fields.description="Donald Trump didn't pull that trigger, and I'm not going to pretend that he did for political purposes. But I was shot, a Secret Service agent is dead, and Donald's response is to go in front of a camera and suggest that maybe the whole thing happened because his campaign was doing well. I don't know what to tell you anymore. The American people can decide for themselves what they think of that.";
  wuaFeedback(1649,"That's about all you need to say. Trump spends the next three days insisting he never accused you of anything. He's a loathsome ghoul, and aside from a few most Republicans seem to think he is too. Dick Cheney even came out to call him reckless, imagine that.");
  third.fields.question=1196;
  third.fields.description="I'm not interested in talking about Donald Trump today. An American died protecting my life last week. There are things more important than an election, and I'm not going to spend my first day out of the hospital entertaining whatever this clown has to say.";
  wuaFeedback(1650,"That's more than respectable. You don't give them anything more to work with. Trump can wallow in his own misery for a few more weeks, you shouldn't feel the need to stop him.");
  campaignTrail_temp.answers_json.filter(x=>Number(x.fields.question)===1196 && ![1649,1650].includes(Number(x.pk)))
    .forEach(x=>x.fields.question=-99001);
  wuaSetGlobalEffect(1649,.040);
  wuaSetGlobalEffect(1650,.035);
  wuaNeutralizeIssueEffects(1649);
  wuaNeutralizeIssueEffects(1650);
  return true;
}
